hat a + c = 2*x => (a + c) even and avg = x. For each a in S, compute c = 2*x - a, check if c in S.\\n\\nThus we can implement check by iterating over y in current set (maybe up to size ~200). This is fine.\\n\\nNow implement greedy: start with base set, then candidates = list(set(range(1,n+1))-set(S)). Shuffle via rng.permutation, iterate, add if safe.\\n\\nReturn list sorted maybe.\\n\\nReturn as list.\\n\\nNow produce final answer with description in braces.\\n\\nLet\'s craft final output."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1589,"prompt_tokens":539,"total_tokens":2128}}'res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-614b06e2354d58b20d8983198d544c37","created":1766037044,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{Greedy construction starting from the base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d Salem\xe2\x80\x91Spencer set, then iteratively add remaining numbers in a deterministic rng\xe2\x80\x91driven order while preserving the 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free property.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from various rng specifications.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    # assume seedable int or array\xe2\x80\x91like\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Return a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\n    The algorithm is deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ---------- 1. Base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d set (guaranteed 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free) ----------\\n    base_set = set()\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        t = x\\n        ok = True\\n        while t:\\n            if t % 3 == 2:          # digit 2 appears \xe2\x86\x92 discard\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            t //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            base_set.add(x)\\n\\n    S = set(base_set)                # current solution\\n    S_lookup = S                     # alias for readability\\n\\n    # ---------- 2. Helper to test if a candidate can be added ----------\\n    def can_add(x, current):\\n        \\"\\"\\"Return True iff adding x to \'current\' yields no 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\"\\"\\"\\n        # quick reject: any y in current such that (x+y) even and mid \xe2\x88\x88 current\\n        for y in current:\\n            if (x + y) & 1:          # sum odd \xe2\x86\x92 no integer midpoint\\n                continue\\n            mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n            if mid in current:\\n                return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in current:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # ---------- 3. Greedy addition of the remaining numbers ----------\\n    remaining = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(remaining)           # deterministic ordering via the rng\\n\\n    for x in remaining:\\n        if can_add(x, S):\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # ---------- 4. Simple local improvement (single\xe2\x80\x91swap) ----------\\n    # Try to replace a conflicting element with a not\xe2\x80\x91selected one if it\\n    # increases the set size (or keeps it equal while improving future adds).\\n    # We perform a bounded number of passes.\\n    not_in_S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in_S)\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_in_S:\\n            # collect all existing elements that would break the AP\xe2\x80\x91free condition\\n            conflicting = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if (x + y) & 1 == 0:\\n                    mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if mid in S:\\n                        conflicting.append(y)\\n                # x as middle\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                    conflicting.append(y)\\n                # x as endpoint (y as middle)\\n                if (y + x) & 1 == 0:\\n                    mid = (y + x) >> 1\\n                    if mid in S:\\n                        conflicting.append(y)\\n                # early stop if many conflicts\\n                if len(conflicting) > 2:\\n                    break\\n            # If at most one conflict we can swap without shrinking the set.\\n            # Swapping keeps size equal; we still do it because it may unblock\\n            # later insertions.\\n            if len(conflicting) == 1:\\n                y_remove = conflicting[0]\\n                S.remove(y_remove)\\n                S.add(x)\\n                not_in_S.remove(x)\\n                not_in_S.append(y_remove)\\n                improved = True\\n                break  # restart scan after any modification\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code implementing heuristic to find large Salem-Spencer set for n=1094. Must be deterministic given rng. Provide algorithm description single sentence inside braces. Then python code.\\n\\nWe need to produce a good large set. Known construction: Use base-3 representation digits 0 or 1 (no digit 2). That gives size approx 2^{log_3 n} ~ n^{log_3 2} \xe2\x89\x88 n^{0.6309}. For n=1094, max size about 1094^0.6309 \xe2\x89\x88 1094^0.63 ~ e^(0.63*ln 1094) \xe2\x89\x88 e^(0.63*6.997) \xe2\x89\x88 e^(4.408) \xe2\x89\x88 82. So about 82. Perhaps we can do better via greedy randomized + local improvement. Could try greedy addition in random order, then hill climbing (attempt to add missing elements by removing a few). But need deterministic.\\n\\nMaybe we can implem