res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-55dc16b51b76c394d4edc6437bbf9507","created":1766037054,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{Greedy randomized construction followed by a deterministic local add\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement that repeatedly tries to insert any remaining element and, if needed, swaps it with an existing one while preserving the 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free property.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Union\\n\\ndef _to_generator(rng: Union[int, np.random.Generator, None]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng()\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng: Union[int, np.random.Generator, None] = None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Returns a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\n    The algorithm works in two phases:\\n      1) Greedy randomized construction (multiple restarts, keep the best).\\n      2) Deterministic local improvement by trying to add or swap missing elements.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_generator(rng)\\n\\n    # fast membership test\\n    def is_compatible(x: int, present: np.ndarray) -> bool:\\n        \\"\\"\\"check whether x can be added to set defined by boolean array present.\\"\\"\\"\\n        # for every y already present, we must have neither 2*y-x nor 2*x-y present\\n        idx_y = np.flatnonzero(present)\\n        # compute potential partners\\n        # vectorised version\\n        partner1 = 2 * idx_y - x\\n        if ((partner1 >= 1) & (partner1 <= n) & present[partner1 - 1]).any():\\n            return False\\n        partner2 = 2 * x - idx_y\\n        if ((partner2 >= 1) & (partner2 <= n) & present[partner2 - 1]).any():\\n            return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    best_set = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)   # boolean mask\\n    best_size = 0\\n\\n    # ----------- Phase 1 : multiple greedy runs -----------------\\n    # number of restarts \xe2\x80\x93 modest, enough for n\xe2\x89\x881100\\n    RESTARTS = 200\\n    for _ in range(RESTARTS):\\n        order = np.arange(1, n + 1)\\n        gen.shuffle(order)\\n        present = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\\n\\n        for x in order:\\n            if is_compatible(x, present):\\n                present[x - 1] = True\\n\\n        cur_size = present.sum()\\n        if cur_size > best_size:\\n            best_size = cur_size\\n            best_set = present.copy()\\n            # early stop if we reach a known bound (theoretical upper bound ~ n/2)\\n            if best_size >= n // 2:\\n                pass\\n\\n    # ----------- Phase 2 : deterministic local improvement -------\\n    present = best_set.copy()\\n    missing = np.flatnonzero(~present) + 1   # list of numbers not in S, 1\xe2\x80\x91based\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        # Try to simply add any missing element that fits\\n        for x in missing:\\n            if is_compatible(x, present):\\n                present[x - 1] = True\\n                improved = True\\n        if improved:\\n            # recompute missing list and continue\\n            missing = np.flatnonzero(~present) + 1\\n            continue\\n\\n        # If simple additions are exhausted, try swaps\\n        # For each missing x, look for a y in S such that swapping keeps AP\xe2\x80\x91free\\n        current_idxs = np.flatnonzero(present) + 1\\n        for x in missing:\\n            # quick filter: x must be compatible with all but possibly one element\\n            # We\'ll test each y individually\\n            for y in current_idxs:\\n                # temporary removal of y\\n                present[y - 1] = False\\n                if is_compatible(x, present):\\n                    # swap accepted\\n                    present[x - 1] = True\\n                    improved = True\\n                    # update lists and break out to outer loop\\n                    current_idxs = np.flatnonzero(present) + 1\\n                    missing = np.flatnonzero(~present) + 1\\n                    break\\n                # restore y and try next\\n                present[y - 1] = True\\n            if improved:\\n                break\\n\\n    # Build the resulting list\\n    result = list(np.flatnonzero(present) + 1)\\n    return result\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output only code? The user says: \\"First, describe your new algorithm and main steps in one sentence. The description must be inside a brace. Next, implement it in Python as a function named heuristic.\\" So produce description inside braces, then code. No additional explanations. Must return a set S for n=1094 but function generic; we try to maximize size. We can use known constructions: Salem-Spencer sets can be built via greedy algorithm with random ordering, maybe using Behrend\'s construction for dense sets, but for n~1000, max size known is around n * exp(-c sqrt(log n)). But we can attempt greedy with random shuffle seeded, then local search.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n- Determine order of candidates: sort by binary representation weight? We can use heuristic: numbers with many 1 bits are less likely to create AP? Not sure.\\n\\nSimpler: Use a greedy algorithm adding numbers in random order, rejecting those that create AP with already selected. Then do a local improvement: try to replace a selected element with a later candidate if it yields larger set.\\n\\nWe should also run multiple restarts with the given rng and keep best.\\n\\nSince n small, we can brute check AP quickly using a boolean array and for each candidate, check for any existing b such that a + c = 2*b. Equivalent to checking for any b in set where (2*b - a) is also in set and > a. Simpler: On adding x, for each y in S, compute z = 2*x - y, if z in S then conflict; also for any y, compute w = 2*y - x if w in S -> conflict. So we can test O(|S|) per candidate.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n- Convert rng to np.random.Generator.\\n- For a fixed number of iterations (maybe 200), create a random permutation of 1..n, then greedy add.\\n- Keep best set found.\\n- Then local improvement: try to add any missing element if possible (no AP); if add, continue; also try swapping: for each missing element x, for each element y in S, try removing y and adding x, check AP; if improves size, do it.\\n\\nGiven n=1094, we can likely get around 300-400 elements (max known is about 300?). Actually known max size for n=1000 is about 320? Let\'s try.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement loops with reasonable limits.\\n\\nReturn best set as list sorted.\\n\\nMake deterministic: iterations fixed number, use rng to shuffle.\\n\\nOk output.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to output only code? The user says: \\"First, describe your new algorithm and main steps in one sentence. The description must be inside a brace. Next, implement it in Python as a function named heuristic.\\" So produce description inside braces, then code. No additional explanations. Must return a set S for n=1094 but function generic; we try to maximize size. We can use known constructions: Salem-Spencer sets can be built via greedy algorithm with random ordering, maybe using Behrend\'s construction for dense sets, but for n~1000, max size known is around n * exp(-c sqrt(log n)). But we can attempt greedy with random shuffle seeded, then local search.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n- Determine order of candidates: sort by binary representation weight? We can use heuristic: numbers with many 1 bits are less likely to create AP? Not sure.\\n\\nSimpler: Use a greedy algorithm adding numbers in random order, rejecting those that create AP with already selected. Then do a local improvement: try to replace a selected element with a later candidate if it yields larger set.\\n\\nWe should also run multiple restarts with the given rng and keep best.\\n\\nSince n small, we can brute check AP quickly using a boolean array and for each candidate, check for any existing b such that a + c = 2*b. Equivalent to checking for any b in set where (2*b - a) is also in set and > a. Simpler: On adding x, for each y in S, compute z = 2*x - y, if z in S then conflict; also for any y, compute w = 2*y - x if w in S -> conflict. So we can test O(|S|) per candidate.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n- Convert rng to np.random.Generator.\\n- For a fixed number of iterations (maybe 200), create a random permutation of 1..n, then greedy add.\\n- Keep best set found.\\n- Then local improvement: try to add any missing element if possible (no AP); if add, continue; also try swapping: for each missing element x, for each element y in S, try removing y and adding x, check AP; if improves size, do it.\\n\\nGiven n=1094, we can likely get around 300-400 elements (max known is about 300?). Actually known max size for n=1000 is about 320? Let\'s try.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement loops with reasonable limits.\\n\\nReturn best set as list sorted.\\n\\nMake deterministic: iterations fixed number, use rng to shuffle.\\n\\nOk output."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1477,"prompt_tokens":539,"total_tokens":2016}}'
