res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-cc8ee81dcf94cb74c0318efb3ca75f3b","created":1766037064,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{Build a maximal 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by repeatedly inserting the currently least\xe2\x80\x91constrained unused element (fewest potential 3\xe2\x80\x91AP partners, ties broken by a deterministic RNG), then run a bounded stochastic local\xe2\x80\x91search that swaps out a single conflicting element whenever the swap does not decrease the cardinality, finally return the resulting set.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Convert rng specification to a NumPy Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    # int, list\xe2\x80\x91like, etc.\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Return a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\n    The algorithm is deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1. Helper: test whether x can be added to the current set S\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    def can_add(x, S):\\n        \\"\\"\\"True iff adding x to S keeps the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free.\\"\\"\\"\\n        for y in S:\\n            # x and y as the two ends of a progression\\n            s = x + y\\n            if s & 1 == 0:                     # even sum \xe2\x86\x92 integer middle\\n                mid = s >> 1\\n                if mid in S:\\n                    return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2. Pre\xe2\x80\x91compute a simple \xe2\x80\x9cconflict degree\xe2\x80\x9d for each number:\\n    #    number of possible mids with any other element in the universe.\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)          # index 0 unused\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        # y runs over all numbers \xe2\x89\xa0 x; we only need to count those\\n        # that give an integer midpoint inside [1,n].\\n        # For a fixed x, midpoint exists iff (x+y) even.\\n        # The count equals the number of y with same parity as x,\\n        # which is floor((n - 1) / 2). For simplicity compute directly.\\n        cnt = 0\\n        for y in range(1, n + 1):\\n            if y == x:\\n                continue\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if 1 <= mid <= n:\\n                    cnt += 1\\n        degree[x] = cnt\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3. Greedy insertion ordered by increasing degree\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    unused = list(range(1, n + 1))\\n    # Shuffle within equal\xe2\x80\x91degree buckets to keep determinism via rng\\n    # First group by degree\\n    buckets = {}\\n    for v in unused:\\n        buckets.setdefault(degree[v], []).append(v)\\n    ordered = []\\n    for d in sorted(buckets):\\n        vals = buckets[d]\\n        gen.shuffle(vals)\\n        ordered.extend(vals)\\n\\n    S = set()\\n    for x in ordered:\\n        if can_add(x, S):\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4. Bounded stochastic local search (swap improvement)\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    not_in_S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in_S)\\n\\n    # number of attempts \xe2\x80\x93 heuristic constant (fast for n\xe2\x89\x881k)\\n    max_iters = 5000\\n    it = 0\\n    while it < max_iters:\\n        it += 1\\n        if not not_in_S:\\n            break\\n        x = not_in_S[gen.integers(len(not_in_S))]   # random candidate\\n\\n        # Find conflicting elements of S with respect to x\\n        conflicts = []\\n        for y in S:\\n            # x and y as ends\\n            s = x + y\\n            if s & 1 == 0:\\n                mid = s >> 1\\n                if mid in S:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                conflicts.append(y)\\n            if len(conflicts) > 1:\\n                break\\n\\n        # No conflict \xe2\x86\x92 safe addition\\n        if not conflicts:\\n            S.add(x)\\n            not_in_S.remove(x)\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Exactly one conflicting element \xe2\x86\x92 try a swap\\n        if len(conflicts) == 1:\\n            y = conflicts[0]\\n            # Perform tentative swap and test validity\\n            S.remove(y)\\n            if can_add(x, S):\\n                S.add(x)\\n                not_in_S.remove(x)\\n                not_in_S.append(y)   # y becomes available again\\n                continue\\n            # swap failed \xe2\x80\x93 revert\\n            S.add(y)\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code implementing a new algorithm, different from provided ones. Must start with description in braces, one sentence. Then implement heuristic with described algorithm.\\n\\nDesign: Use a greedy construction based on graph coloring: treat numbers as vertices, edges between numbers that would form a 3-AP with a third already chosen. Use a maximal independent set via a heuristic ordering by degree (small degree first). More concrete: compute for each x its conflict set with others (pairs that with a third would create AP). But we can use heuristic: order numbers by random permutation (deterministic) then iteratively add if no conflict, but that\'s similar to previous. Need different: Use a greedy algorithm that builds set by scanning residues modulo a prime p (e.g., p=5) and picks numbers with certain residues that avoid AP within same residue class using van der Waerden bounds. Maybe use construction known as Salem-Spencer set using base-4 no-2? But still similar.\\n\\nAlternative: Use a SAT-like local search: start with random subset of size target, then fix violations by flipping bits (like WalkSAT). That would be different.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement: start with empty S. For each x in 1..n in order of decreasing \\"score\\" where score = number of potential APs it participates in (less is better) compute a random tie-breaker via rng. Then attempt to add x if no conflict. After initial pass, perform a local improvement: while there exists a violating triple (a,b,c) with a,c in S and b not, we can try to add b by removing one of a or c if it reduces violations, using a limited budget.\\n\\nImplementation steps:\\n\\n- Precompute for each x list of pairs (y,z) such that x is middle of AP: y = x - d, z = x + d within range.\\n Also x as endpoint: for each d, y = x + 2d (if <=n) where x is left endpoint and middle would be x+d .\\nBut maybe easier: have function conflicts(x, S) that returns True if any y in S such that (x+y)%2==0 and mid in S etc. same as earlier; but we need scanning for violations after.\\n\\nLocal improvement: iterate through numbers not in S, attempt to add them if it would create at most one conflict; if conflict, compute which conflicting element to drop and add if net size not decrease.\\n\\nThat\'s similar to earlier though. Need distinct algorithm.\\n\\nMaybe use \\"randomized greedy with backtracking\\": maintain stack, when cannot add next candidate, backtrack and try alternative.\\n\\nBut we just need different.\\n\\nI\'ll propose: Use maximal independent set on graph where vertices are numbers and edges connect numbers that together would force a third also possibly in set? Actually AP constraint is hypergraph of triples; we can approximate by building conflict graph edges between any two numbers that together with a third within range would form AP (i.e., if (a+c) even then b=(a+c)/2). Then any edge indicates that both cannot be present together if middle also present, but they could be present if middle absent. Not correct.\\n\\nBetter: Use iterative deepening: start with base-3 no-2 set; then perform simulated annealing: random swaps to improve size.\\n\\nBut they said different from given which already includes base-3 and swaps; we could avoid base-3, start with empty and random sequential addition guided by score.\\n\\nSo description: \\"{Build a maximal 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by repeatedly inserting the currently least\xe2\x80\x91constrained unused element (fewest potential AP conflicts), breaking ties with a deterministic RNG, and then perform a bounded stochastic local\xe2\x80\x91search that swaps out a conflicting element when it yields a net gain.}\\" That is distinct.\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n- Compute for each x the number of APs it participates in with any other y (i.e., count of d such that x-d >=1 or x+d <=n). That\'s just max distance. Simpler: degree = number of y such that (x+y) even and (x+y)//2 in [1,n]. That\'s count of possible middles with other endpoint.\\n\\n- Sort candidates by degree ascending, tie shuffle.\\n\\n- Greedy add if can_add.\\n\\n- Then local search: limited iterations (e.g., 5000). At each iteration pick random x not in S, compute conflicts with S (list of y causing AP). If len(conflicts)==1, replace y with x. If len(conflicts)==0, just add. Keep track.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nSeems okay.\\n\\nLet\'s write code. Ensure deterministic RNG derived from rng.\\n\\nComplexities O(n^2) maybe okay for n=1094.\\n\\nImplement can_add function similar.\\n\\nLocal search loop limited by budget.\\n\\nReturn list sorted.\\n\\nNow produce final answer with description braces then code.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to output code implementing a new algorithm, different from provided ones. Must start with description in braces, one sentence. Then implement heuristic with described algorithm.\\n\\nDesign: Use a greedy construction based on graph coloring: treat numbers as vertices, edges between numbers that would form a 3-AP with a third already chosen. Use a maximal independent set via a heuristic ordering by degree (small degree first). More concrete: compute for each x its conflict set with others (pairs that with a third would create AP). But we can use heuristic: order numbers by random permutation (deterministic) then iteratively add if no conflict, but that\'s similar to previous. Need different: Use a greedy algorithm that builds set by scanning residues modulo a prime p (e.g., p=5) and picks numbers with certain residues that avoid AP within same residue class using van der Waerden bounds. Maybe use construction known as Salem-Spencer set using base-4 no-2? But still similar.\\n\\nAlternative: Use a SAT-like local search: start with random subset of size target, then fix violations by flipping bits (like WalkSAT). That would be different.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement: start with empty S. For each x in 1..n in order of decreasing \\"score\\" where score = number of potential APs it participates in (less is better) compute a random tie-breaker via rng. Then attempt to add x if no conflict. After initial pass, perform a local improvement: while there exists a violating triple (a,b,c) with a,c in S and b not, we can try to add b by removing one of a or c if it reduces violations, using a limited budget.\\n\\nImplementation steps:\\n\\n- Precompute for each x list of pairs (y,z) such that x is middle of AP: y = x - d, z = x + d within range