articipates in with any other y (i.e., count of d such that x-d >=1 or x+d <=n). That\'s just max distance. Simpler: degree = number of y such that (x+y) even and (x+y)//2 in [1,n]. That\'s count of possible middles with other endpoint.\\n\\n- Sort candidates by degree ascending, tie shuffle.\\n\\n- Greedy add if can_add.\\n\\n- Then local search: limited iterations (e.g., 5000). At each iteration pick random x not in S, compute conflicts with S (list of y causing AP). If len(conflicts)==1, replace y with x. If len(conflicts)==0, just add. Keep track.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nSeems okay.\\n\\nLet\'s write code. Ensure deterministic RNG derived from rng.\\n\\nComplexities O(n^2) maybe okay for n=1094.\\n\\nImplement can_add function similar.\\n\\nLocal search loop limited by budget.\\n\\nReturn list sorted.\\n\\nNow produce final answer with description braces then code."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":2090,"prompt_tokens":2621,"total_tokens":4711}}'
res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-fbede391e0adfe66764b6d40c0624988","created":1766037064,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{Iteratively delete the element that participates in the most currently complete three\xe2\x80\x91term arithmetic progressions, then greedily re\xe2\x80\x91insert removed numbers in a deterministic random order while preserving the 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free property.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from various rng specifications.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Construct a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (n=1094 in practice).\\n\\n    The algorithm proceeds in two phases:\\n    1) repeatedly remove the element that lies in the largest number of\\n       presently\xe2\x80\x91complete three\xe2\x80\x91term arithmetic progressions;\\n    2) try to re\xe2\x80\x91add the removed elements in a deterministic random order\\n       whenever doing so does not create a new progression.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1) Pre\xe2\x80\x91compute all 3\xe2\x80\x91AP triples (a,b,c) with a<b<c and a+c=2*b\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    triples = []\\n    for b in range(2, n):\\n        a_max = b - 1\\n        for a in range(1, a_max + 1):\\n            c = 2 * b - a\\n            if c > n:\\n                continue\\n            triples.append((a, b, c))\\n    triples = np.array(triples, dtype=np.int32)          # shape (m,3)\\n    m = triples.shape[0]\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2) Initialise all numbers as present, all triples as active\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    present = np.ones(n + 1, dtype=bool)                 # index 0 unused\\n    active = np.ones(m, dtype=bool)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3) Deletion loop: remove element with highest conflict count\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    while True:\\n        # triples that are still fully present\\n        good = (active &\\n                present[triples[:, 0]] &\\n                present[triples[:, 1]] &\\n                present[triples[:, 2]])\\n        idx = np.nonzero(good)[0]\\n        if idx.size == 0:\\n            break\\n\\n        # count how many such triples each element belongs to\\n        cnt = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=np.int32)\\n        np.add.at(cnt, triples[idx].ravel(), 1)\\n\\n        maxc = cnt.max()\\n        cand = np.flatnonzero(cnt == maxc)               # includes 0 which we ignore\\n        cand = cand[cand != 0]                           # drop index 0\\n        gen.shuffle(cand)\\n        remove = int(cand[0])\\n\\n        # delete the chosen element\\n        present[remove] = False\\n\\n        # deactivate all triples that contain the removed element\\n        contains = np.any(triples == remove, axis=1)\\n        active[contains] = False\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4) Greedy re\xe2\x80\x91insertion of removed elements\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    current_set = set(np.flatnonzero(present)[1:])        # drop the dummy 0\\n    removed = list(set(range(1, n + 1)) - current_set)\\n    gen.shuffle(removed)\\n\\n    def can_add(x, S):\\n        \\"\\"\\"True iff adding x keeps the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free.\\"\\"\\"\\n        for y in S:\\n            s = x + y\\n            if s & 1:          # odd sum \xe2\x86\x92 no integer midpoint\\n                continue\\n            mid = s >> 1\\n            if mid in S:\\n                return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    for x in removed:\\n        if can_add(x, current_set):\\n            current_set.add(x)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 5) Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n    return sorted(current_set)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a new algorithm different from given ones. Must describe in one sentence inside braces. Then implement Python function heuristic with described steps. Need to output S as list/tuple. Must ensure 3-AP-free. Look for large set for n=1094. We can try to use known constructions: Salem-Spencer set up to n can be of size about n / (log n). For n=1094, maximum known is around n / (sqrt(log n)). But we can attempt to produce a set using greedy algorithm with priority based on binary representation parity maybe, but need differ from previous: previous builds base-3 no-2 then greedy addition with swaps. New approach: Use randomized iterative removal (like start with full set then remove minimal elements to break APs). For example start with all numbers, while there is a 3-AP, remove the middle element (or random among the three). That yields a maximal 3-AP-free set might be decent. Additionally incorporate deterministic random order using rng.\\n\\nAlgorithm: Start with candidate set all numbers; compute all AP triples; while any AP exists, pick one triple using rng order, remove the element that participates in most APs (heuristic) to break many triples. After no APs left, we have a maximal set; then we can try to add back any removed numbers that now fit (greedy addition). This is different.\\n\\nImplement efficiently: For each element maintain a set of APs it is part of. Since n only 1094, we can precompute all triples (a,b,c) with a<b<c, a+c=2b. That\'s O(n^2). That\'s about 600k combos, fine.\\n\\nWe\'ll maintain a boolean array in_set. We\'ll also maintain a count of conflicts per element (how many APs where it\'s middle). Actually any AP is broken if any of its three elements missing. We\'ll aim to remove middle elements because they are in many APs. We\'ll iterate: compute for each element how many APs currently fully present (i.e., all three present). Then choose element with highest count, remove it. Continue until no fully present AP.\\n\\nImplementation steps:\\n\\n- Build list of triples (a,b,c). Use loops: for b in 2..n-1, for a in 1..b-1, c = 2*b - a, if c<=n then add triple.\\n\\n- Represent each triple as tuple (a,b,c).\\n\\n- Keep a set of active triples (still fully present). Initially all triples.\\n\\n- Maintain array present[1..n] = True.\\n\\n- While active triples not empty:\\n   - Compute conflict count for each element: for each triple in active, increment count for its three elements (or just middle but we may remove any). To be deterministic, we recompute each iteration (n small).\\n   - Choose element with highest count; tie break by rng order (shuffle indices then pick first with max).\\n   - Remove that element: present[x]=False.\\n   - Remove all triples that contain x from active (filter).\\n\\n- After loop, we have a set S.\\n\\n- Then try greedy addition of removed elements in random order: iterate over removed elements shuffled, if adding element does not create AP (check against current set using can_add similar), add.\\n\\n- Return sorted list.\\n\\nComplexities: O(num_triples * iterations). Worst-case might be O(n^3). But n=1094, triples about ~ n^2/4 ~ 300k. Each iteration we recalc counts; number of removals maybe about n/2 ~ 500. So 500*300k = 150M, might be okay in Python? Might be borderline but okay with optimization using arrays.\\n\\nWe can store for each element list of triple indices it belongs to, to update counts incrementally. Simpler: just recompute counts using list of triples and checking present for each element quickly.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement using numpy arrays for speed.\\n\\nImplementation details:\\n\\n- triples = np.empty((m,3), dtype=np.int32)\\n\\n- present = np.ones(n+1, dtype=bool)\\n\\n- active = np.ones(m, dtype=bool)\\n\\nLoop:\\n\\nwhile True:\\n    # find triples where all three present\\n    idx = np.where(active & present[triples[:,0]] & present[triples[:,1]] & present[triples[:,2]])[0]\\n    if idx.size==0: break\\n    # count occurrences\\n    cnt = np.zeros(n+1, dtype=np.int32)\\n    np.add.at(cnt, triples[idx,:].ravel(), 1)\\n    # choose max count element\\n    maxc = cnt.max()\\n    candidates = np.flatnonzero(cnt==maxc)\\n    # deterministic random tie break\\n    rng.shuffle(candidates)\\n    remove = candidates[0]\\n    present[remove]=False\\n    # deactivate any triple containing remove\\n    affected = np.any(triples==remove, axis=1)\\n    active[affected]=False\\n\\nAfter loop, S = np.flatnonzero(present[1:]) +1? Actually present array index.\\n\\nRemoved elements list = np.flatnonzero(~present[1:]) +1.\\n\\nThen greedy add back:\\n\\nremoved = list of removed elements; rng.shuffle(removed)\\n\\nDefine can_add using set for fast lookup.\\n\\nAdd if no AP conflict.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nMake deterministic RNG: use _to_rng similar.\\n\\nLet\'s craft final answer.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to produce a new algorithm different from given ones. Must describe in one sentence inside braces. Then implement Python function heuristic with described steps. Need to output S as list/tuple. Must ensure 3-AP-free. Look for large set for n=1094. We can try to use known constructions: Salem-Spencer set up to n can be of size about n / (log n). For n=1094, maximum known is around n / (sqrt(log n)). But we can attempt to produce a set using greedy algorithm with priority based on binary representation parity maybe, but need differ from previous: previous builds base-3 no-2 then greedy addition with swaps. New approach: Use randomized iterative removal (like start with full set then remove minimal elements to break APs). For example start with all numbers, while there is a 3-AP, remove the middle element (or random among the three). That yields a maximal 3-AP-free set might be decent. Additionally incorporate deterministic random order using rng.\\n\\nAlgorithm: Start with candidate set all numbers; compute all AP triples; while any AP exists, pick one triple using rng order, remove the element that participates in most APs (heuristic) to break many triples. After no APs left, we have a maximal set; then we can try to add back any removed numbers that now fit (greedy addition). This is different.\\n\\nImplement efficiently: For each element maintain a set of APs it is part of. Since n only 1094, we can precompute all triples (a,b,c) with a<b<c, a+c=2b. That\'s O(n^2). That\'s about 600k combos, fine.\\n\\nWe\'ll maintain a boolean array in_set. We\'ll also maintain a count of conflicts per element (how many APs where it\'s middle). Actually any AP is broken if any of its three elements missing. We\'ll aim to remove middle elements because they are in many APs. We\'ll iterate: compute for each element how many APs currently fully present (i.e., all three present). Then choose element with highest count, remove it. Continue until no fully present AP.\\n\\nImplementation steps:\\n\\n- Build list of triples (a,b,c). Use loops: for b in 2..n-1, for a in 1..b-1, c = 2*b - a, if c<=n then add triple.\\n\\n- Represent each triple as tuple (a,b,c).\\n\\n- Keep a set of active triples (still fully present). Initially all triples.\\n\\n- Maintain array present[1..n] = True.\\n\\n- While active triples not empty:\\n   - Compute conflict count for each element: for each triple in active, increment count for its three elements (or just middle but we may remove any). To be deterministic, we recompute each iteration (n small).\\n   - Choose element with highest count; tie break by rng order (shuffle indices then pick first with max).\\n   - Remove that element: present[x]=False.\\n   - Remove all triples that contain x from active (filter).\\n\\n- After loop, we have a set S.\\n\\n- Then try greedy addition of removed elements in random order: iterate over removed elements shuffled, if adding element does not create AP (check against current set using can_add similar), add.\\n\\n- Return sorted list.\\n\\nComplexities: O(num_triples * iterations). Worst-case might be O(n^3). But n=1094, triples about ~ n^2/4 ~ 300k. Each iteration we recalc counts; number of removals maybe about n/2 ~ 500. So 500*300k = 150M, might be okay in Python? Might be borderline but okay with optimization using arrays.\\n\\nWe can store for each element list of triple indices it belongs to, to update counts incrementally. Simpler: just recompute counts using list of triples and checking present for each element quickly.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement using numpy arrays for speed.\\n\\nImplementation details:\\n\\n- triples = np.empty((m,3), dtype=np.int32)\\n\\n- present = np.ones(n+1, dtype=bool)\\n\\n- active = np.ones(m, dtype=bool)\\n\\nLoop:\\n\\nwhile True:\\n    # find triples where all three present\\n    idx = np.where(active & present[triples[:,0]] & present[triples[:,1]] & present[triples[:,2]])[0]\\n    if idx.size==0: break\\n    # count occurrences\\n    cnt = np.zeros(n+1, dtype=np.int32)\\n    np.add.at(cnt, triples[idx,:].ravel(), 1)\\n    # choose max count element\\n    maxc = cnt.max()\\n    candidates = np.flatnonzero(cnt==maxc)\\n    # deterministic random tie break\\n    rng.shuffle(candidates)\\n    remove = candidates[0]\\n    present[remove]=False\\n    # deactivate any triple containing remove\\n    affected = np.any(triples==remove, axis=1)\\n    active[affected]=False\\n\\nAfter loop, S = np.flatnonzero(present[1:]) +1? Actually present array index.\\n\\nRemoved elements list = np.flatnonzero(~present[1:]) +1.\\n\\nThen greedy add back:\\n\\nremoved = list of removed elements; rng.shuffle(removed)\\n\\nDefine can_add using set for fast lookup.\\n\\nAdd if no AP conflict.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nMake deterministic RNG: use _to_rng similar.\\n\\nLet\'s craft final answer."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":2013,"prompt_tokens":2621,"total_tokens":4634}}' Obj:  None| Obj:  -91.0|
