res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-b10a29ff935ada3655b32152b1412093","created":1766037094,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"**Common backbone idea:**  \\nBoth reference methods first build a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by a greedy construction (ordered insertion) and then try to improve it with a short, deterministic, stochastic local\xe2\x80\x91search that swaps or adds elements without decreasing the cardinality.\\n\\n**New algorithm (one\xe2\x80\x91sentence description):**  \\n{Iteratively prune the whole universe by repeatedly deleting the element that participates in the most remaining three\xe2\x80\x91term arithmetic progressions, then greedily re\xe2\x80\x91insert any deleted elements that can be added without creating a progression.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Convert various rng specifications to a NumPy Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Return a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (n is expected to be 1094).\\n    The algorithm is deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1. Enumerate all 3\xe2\x80\x91AP triples (a,b,c) with a<b<c\xe2\x89\xa4n, a+c=2b.\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    triples = []                     # list of (a,b,c)\\n    element_triples = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]   # indices of triples per element\\n    for a in range(1, n + 1):\\n        for c in range(a + 2, n + 1, 2):   # a and c must have same parity\\n            b = (a + c) // 2\\n            if b > a and b < c:\\n                idx = len(triples)\\n                triples.append((a, b, c))\\n                element_triples[a].append(idx)\\n                element_triples[b].append(idx)\\n                element_triples[c].append(idx)\\n\\n    m = len(triples)\\n    triple_active = np.ones(m, dtype=bool)     # whether the triple is still fully present\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2. Initial counts: how many active triples each element belongs to.\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    counts = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=int)\\n    for v in range(1, n + 1):\\n        counts[v] = len(element_triples[v])\\n\\n    present = np.ones(n + 1, dtype=bool)       # element still in the candidate set\\n    present[0] = False                         # unused index\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3. Prune by repeatedly removing the most conflicted element.\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    while True:\\n        # find any active triple\\n        active_idxs = np.nonzero(triple_active)[0]\\n        if active_idxs.size == 0:\\n            break\\n\\n        # element with maximal count among currently present elements\\n        max_cnt = counts[present].max()\\n        candidates = np.nonzero((counts == max_cnt) & present)[0]\\n        # deterministic tie\xe2\x80\x91break via rng\\n        if candidates.size > 1:\\n            choice = gen.integers(len(candidates))\\n            v_remove = candidates[choice]\\n        else:\\n            v_remove = candidates[0]\\n\\n        # remove v_remove\\n        present[v_remove] = False\\n\\n        # deactivate all triples that contain v_remove\\n        for t_idx in element_triples[v_remove]:\\n            if not triple_active[t_idx]:\\n                continue\\n            a, b, c = triples[t_idx]\\n            triple_active[t_idx] = False\\n            # decrement counts of the other two vertices if they are still present\\n            for u in (a, b, c):\\n                if u != v_remove and present[u]:\\n                    counts[u] -= 1\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4. Greedy re\xe2\x80\x91insertion of removed elements (if possible).\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    removed = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(removed)\\n\\n    # helper to test insertion\\n    def can_add(x):\\n        \\"\\"\\"Return True iff x can be added to the current set without creating a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\"\\"\\"\\n        for y in np.nonzero(present)[0]:\\n            # x and y as ends\\n            s = x + y\\n            if s & 1 == 0:\\n                mid = s >> 1\\n                if present[mid]:\\n                    return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    for x in removed:\\n        if can_add(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 5. Return the resulting set (sorted list for reproducibility).\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if present[v]]\\n    return S\\n```\\n","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to produce answer: identify common backbone idea, then one-sentence description in braces, then implement new algorithm. Must not give extra explanation. Provide concise output.\\n\\nBackbone idea: greedy construction based on ordering plus local search swaps. New algorithm should be different but motivated: maybe use randomized iterative removal (starting from full set, iteratively delete elements that appear in many 3-APs) \xe2\x80\x93 a \\"conflict removal\\" approach. That is different from insertion greedy. So description in braces: something like \\"{Iteratively prune the universe by repeatedly removing the element participating in the greatest number of remaining 3\xe2\x80\x91APs, then perform a limited forward\xe2\x80\x91addition sweep to recover any deletable spots.}\\". Implementation deterministic.\\n\\nImplement: start with all numbers 1..n. Compute for each element count of 3-APs where it is middle or endpoint with both other endpoints still present. While any 3-AP exists, remove element with max count (break ties via rng). After removal loop ends, we have AP-free set. Then try to add back any removed elements that can be reinserted (greedy) to increase size. Optionally small swap.\\n\\nComplexity: O(n^2) naive, but n~1094, okay. We can precompute all AP triples (a,b,c). For each triple, store list. Then counts maintain. Upon removal, decrement counts for related triples. Implement efficiently.\\n\\nLet\'s code.\\n\\nSteps:\\n- Generate list of all triples (a,b,c) with a<b<c, a + c = 2*b, within 1..n.\\n- For each element, maintain set of triple indices it belongs to.\\n- active set initially all elements present. active triples indices where all three present.\\n- While there exists active triple:\\n   - Compute for each element its current conflict count = number of active triples containing it.\\n   - Choose element with max count (if multiple, use rng.choice among them).\\n   - Remove element from active set; mark removed.\\n   - For each triple containing that element, deactivate it (remove from active triples). Also decrement counts for other two elements.\\n   - Loop.\\n\\nWe can keep counts array updated incrementally: start counts = total triples each element appears in. When a triple is deactivated, decrement counts of its other two elements (if still present). We\'ll need data structures: triples list of (a,b,c). element_triples: list of list of triple indices.\\n\\nActive flag for each triple: bool array.\\n\\nRemoved flag for elements.\\n\\nLoop until no active triples.\\n\