res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-29fef3b6589a29a20146291d5388a24a","created":1766037093,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"**Backbone idea:** both methods grow a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by repeatedly inserting a \xe2\x80\x9cleast\xe2\x80\x91constrained\xe2\x80\x9d element (according to a simple conflict score), then try single\xe2\x80\x91element swaps to improve the solution.\\n\\n**One\xe2\x80\x91sentence description:** {Iteratively pick the currently unused number with the smallest number of 3\xe2\x80\x91AP conflicts, insert it if it does not clash with the existing set, update conflict scores, and finally perform a bounded single\xe2\x80\x91swap local\xe2\x80\x91search using the same deterministic random order.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from various rng specifications.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Construct a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (here n=1094) using a\\n    min\xe2\x80\x91conflict greedy builder followed by a short single\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement.\\n    Returns a list of selected integers.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1. Build the conflict graph: two numbers are adjacent if they could\\n    #    appear together in a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP with a third number inside [1,n].\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    neighbours = [set() for _ in range(n + 1)]   # index 0 unused\\n    for a in range(1, n + 1):\\n        # a as an endpoint, partner b gives integer midpoint m\\n        # (a+b) even -> m = (a+b)//2 . If m in [1,n] we add edge a\xe2\x80\x93b.\\n        step = 2 if a % 2 == 0 else 2   # just to iterate b of same parity\\n        for b in range(a + 1, n + 1, 2):\\n            s = a + b\\n            if s & 1:   # sum odd \xe2\x86\x92 no integer midpoint\\n                continue\\n            m = s >> 1\\n            if 1 <= m <= n:\\n                neighbours[a].add(b)\\n                neighbours[b].add(a)\\n\\n        # a as the middle element: for any other b, the third point is c = 2*a - b\\n        for b in range(1, n + 1):\\n            if b == a:\\n                continue\\n            c = 2 * a - b\\n            if 1 <= c <= n:\\n                # (b, a, c) is a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP, so b and c are conflicting\\n                neighbours[a].add(b)\\n                neighbours[b].add(a)\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2. Initialise degree (conflict count) for each unused vertex\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)\\n    for v in range(1, n + 1):\\n        degree[v] = len(neighbours[v])\\n\\n    unused = set(range(1, n + 1))\\n    S = set()\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3. Greedy insertion of minimum\xe2\x80\x91degree vertices\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    while unused:\\n        # find current minimum degree among unused vertices\\n        min_deg = min(degree[v] for v in unused)\\n        # collect all candidates with that degree\\n        cand = [v for v in unused if degree[v] == min_deg]\\n        gen.shuffle(cand)                # deterministic tie\xe2\x80\x91break\\n        v = cand[0]\\n\\n        # check if v conflicts with any already selected element\\n        conflict = any(u in S for u in neighbours[v])\\n        if not conflict:\\n            S.add(v)\\n\\n        # remove v from further consideration\\n        unused.remove(v)\\n\\n        # update degrees of remaining unused neighbours\\n        for w in neighbours[v]:\\n            if w in unused:\\n                degree[w] -= 1\\n\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4. Short single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement (keep size, improve future adds)\\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------\\n    not_in_S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in_S)\\n\\n    max_swaps = 2000\\n    swaps = 0\\n    for x in not_in_S:\\n        if swaps >= max_swaps:\\n            break\\n        # collect conflicting members of S with respect to x\\n        conflicts = [y for y in neighbours[x] if y in S]\\n        if len(conflicts) == 1:\\n            y = conflicts[0]\\n            # try swap y -> x\\n            S.remove(y)\\n            # verify x now fits\\n            if all(z not in neighbours[x] for z in S):\\n                S.add(x)\\n                swaps += 1\\n                # update not_in_S list for future iterations\\n                not_in_S.remove(x)\\n                not_in_S.append(y)\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to produce answer with three parts: identify common backbone idea, then one-sentence description inside braces, then implement new algorithm in python. No extra explanations, only required output. Must not add additional text.\\n\\nCommon backbone idea: both algorithms start with a seed 3-AP-free set (greedy or base-3) and then add elements in a particular order checking can_add, with local swaps. So backbone: incremental constructive building with conflict checking and local improvement.\\n\\nOne sentence description inside braces: e.g., \\"{Construct a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by iteratively selecting the element with fewest currently forbidden 3\xe2\x80\x91AP extensions, inserting it, and periodically performing single\xe2\x80\x91swap repairs guided by a deterministic random order.}\\"\\n\\nImplement new algorithm different form but motivated: maybe use graph coloring approach: treat numbers as vertices, edges between pairs that would create AP with a third number already selected; we can do maximal independent set via greedy with degree ordering (like largest independent set). That\'s different but similar backbone.\\n\\nImplement: compute for each candidate its degree (number of conflicts with other unused numbers) dynamically, pick min-degree each step, add if no conflict (since we maintain set). After construction, do local improvement swapping as before.\\n\\nImplement deterministic rng for ties.\\n\\nReturn list sorted.\\n\\nLet\'s code efficient conflict detection: For candidate x, conflict with S if exists y in S such that (x+y) even and mid in S or 2*x - y in S.\\n\\nWe\'ll maintain S set.\\n\\nAlgorithm steps:\\n\\n1. Precompute adjacency between any two numbers that would form a 3-AP with a third within [1,n]: for each unordered pair (a,b), compute mid = (a+b)//2 if sum even; if mid integer and within range, then a and b conflict via mid. Also, a and c conflict if a is middle of b and c: i.e., for each a,b, compute c=2*a-b, if c in range, then a and b conflict. Basically conflict graph is symmetric.\\n\\nBut O(n^2) for n~1094 acceptable.\\n\\nWe\'ll store for each v a list of neighbors.\\n\\nThen algorithm:\\n\\n- Initialize all vertices unused.\\n\\n- Compute degree = len(neighbors[v]) (or maybe number of conflicts with other unused?)\\n\\n- While unused not empty:\\n\\n   pick v with minimal degree among unused (break ties by rng shuffle order). If v can be added (i.e., no neighbor already in S), add to S, remove v from unused, and also remove from neighbor lists: for each w neighbor of v, decrement degree[w] if w still unused.\\n\\n   else (if conflict with S) just remove v from unused (skip) maybe.\\n\\nActually if neighbor in S then cannot add; but we could still keep it for later swaps. For simplicity, we just skip adding and mark as unavailable.\\n\\nAfter greedy, run local swaps: for each x not in S, attempt to swap with a conflicting y if size not decrease.\\n\\nImplement local swap: find conflicts (neighbors that are in S). If exactly one conflict, try swapping.\\n\\nWe need neighbor sets for quick conflict detection.\\n\\nComplexities okay.\\n\\nImplement deterministic tie-breaking: gather list of vertices with min degree, shuffle with rng, pick first.\\n\\nImplement degree updating.\\n\\nOk produce final code.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to produce answer with three parts: identify common backbone idea, then one-sentence description inside braces, then implement new algorithm in python. No extra explanations, only required output. Must not add additional text.\\n\\nCommon backbone idea: both algorithms start with a seed 3-AP-free set (greedy or base-3) and then add elements in a particular order checking can_add, with local swaps. So backbone: incremental constructive building with conflict checking and local improvement.\\n\\nOne sentence description inside braces: e.g., \\"{Construct a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set by iteratively selecting the element with fewest currently forbidden 3\xe2\x80\x91AP extensions, inserting it, and periodically performing single\xe2\x80\x91swap repairs guided by a deterministic random order.}\\"\\n\\nImplement new algorithm different form but motivated: maybe use graph coloring approach: treat numbers as vertices, edges between pairs that would create AP with a third number already selected; we can do maximal independent set via greedy with degree ordering (like largest independent set). That\'s different but similar backbone.\\n\\nImplement: compute for each candidate its degree (number of conflicts with other unused numbers) dynamically, pick min-degree each step, add if no conflict (since we maintain set). After construction, do local improvement swapping as before.\\n\\nImplement deterministic rng for ties.\\n\\nReturn list sorted.\\n\\nLet\'s code efficient conflict detection: For candidate x, conflict with S if exists y in S such that (x+y) even and mid in S or 2*x - y in S.\\n\\nWe\'ll maintain S set.\\n\\nAlgorithm steps:\\n\\n1. Precompute adjacency between any two numbers that would form a 3-AP with a third within [1,n]: for each unordered pair (a,b), compute mid = (a+b)//2 if sum even; if mid integer and within range, then a and b conflict via mid. Also, a and c conflict if a is middle of b and c: i.e., for each a,b, compute c=2*a-b, if c in range, then a and b conflict. Basically conflict graph is symmetric.\\n\\nBut O(n^2) for n~1094 acceptable.\\n\\nWe\'ll store for each v a list of neighbors.\\n\\nThen algorithm:\\n\\n- Initialize all vertices unused.\\n\\n- Compute degree = len(neighbors[v]) (or maybe number of conflicts with other unused?)\\n\\n- While unused not empty:\\n\\n   pick v with minimal degree among unused (break ties by rng shuffle order). If v can be added (i.e., no neighbor already in S), add to S, remove v from unused, and also remove from neighbor lists: for each w neighbor of v, decrement degree[w] if w still unused.\\n\\n   else (if conflict with S) just remove v from unused (skip) maybe.\\n\\nActually if neighbor in S then cannot add; but we could still keep it for later swaps. For simplicity, we just skip adding and mark as unavailable.\\n\\nAfter greedy, run local swaps: for each x not in S, attempt to swap with a conflicting y if size not decrease.\\n\\nImplement local swap: find conflicts (neighbors that are in S). If exactly one conflict, try swapping.\\n\\nWe need neighbor sets for quick conflict detection.\\n\\nComplexities okay.\\n\\nImplement deterministic tie-breaking: gather list of vertices with min degree, shuffle with rng, pick first.\\n\\nImplement degree updating.\\n\\nOk produce final code."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":