res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-29f8a2a91aaffb057e7e65083eda9f3a","created":1766037109,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cCantor\xe2\x80\x9d seed (all numbers whose ternary representation contains only 0/1) is built first, then the remaining numbers are shuffled with a deterministic RNG and greedily inserted whenever they keep the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from None, seed, or Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Construct a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (intended for n=1094).\\n\\n    The algorithm:\\n        1. Seed the set with all numbers whose base\xe2\x80\x913 representation\\n           contains no digit \xe2\x80\x982\xe2\x80\x99 (the classic Cantor construction, which is\\n           AP\xe2\x80\x91free).\\n        2. Shuffle the remaining numbers with a deterministic RNG and\\n           greedily insert each number that does not create a new 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ---------- 1. Cantor seed ----------\\n    present = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=bool)      # index 0 unused\\n    for v in range(1, n + 1):\\n        t = v\\n        ok = True\\n        while t:\\n            if t % 3 == 2:        # digit 2 appears \xe2\x86\x92 not allowed\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            t //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            present[v] = True\\n\\n    # ---------- 2. Greedy addition ----------\\n    # list of numbers not yet present\\n    remaining = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    remaining = np.array(remaining, dtype=int)\\n    gen.shuffle(remaining)\\n\\n    # helper: can we add x without creating a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP?\\n    def can_add(x):\\n        # x as an endpoint\\n        # we need y present such that (x+y) even and mid present\\n        # vectorised over present indices\\n        idxs = np.nonzero(present)[0]\\n        if idxs.size == 0:\\n            return True\\n        # endpoint case\\n        s = x + idxs\\n        even = (s & 1) == 0\\n        mids = s[even] >> 1\\n        if mids.size and present[mids].any():\\n            return False\\n        # middle case: check z = 2*x - y\\n        z = 2 * x - idxs\\n        mask = (z >= 1) & (z <= n)\\n        if mask.any() and present[z[mask]].any():\\n            return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    for x in remaining:\\n        if can_add(int(x)):\\n            present[int(x)] = True\\n\\n    # ---------- Return ----------\\n    S = [i for i in range(1, n + 1) if present[i]]\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output an answer: description in braces, then code implementing new algorithm, distinct from original but may be modified version. Should aim for large set for n=1094. Known best known Salem-Spencer for n up to 1100? The construction using base-3 representation: take numbers whose ternary representation contains no digit \'2\' (i.e., avoid digit 2). This set is AP-free (since it\'s a Cantor set). The size is about n^{log_2(2)/log_2(3)}? Actually size ~ n^{log_2(2)/log_2(3)}? Wait: For numbers up to 3^k, the set of numbers with only digits 0 and 1 in base 3 has size 2^k, which is AP-free. For n=1094 (<3^7=2187), we can take all numbers <1095 with no digit 2 in base 3. That yields size? Let\'s compute quickly: numbers from 0 to 1094 inclusive (including 0) with digits 0/1 only. Count numbers with up to 6 digits (since 3^6=729, 3^7=2187). Actually we need up to 1094.\\n\\nWe could generate set = {x in 1..n : no digit \'2\' in base 3 representation}.