res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-5809a3bc78b32f76ca40e931dc1c6302","created":1766037123,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A two\xe2\x80\x91phase greedy algorithm that starts from the base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d set, then inserts remaining numbers ordered by increasing conflict count and finally performs single\xe2\x80\x91element swaps that resolve a single conflict to enlarge the set.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Set, Iterable\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef _base3_no2(n: int) -> Set[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return the classic Salem\xe2\x80\x93Spencer set obtained from numbers\\n    whose ternary representation contains no digit 2.\\"\\"\\"\\n    s = set()\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        t = x\\n        ok = True\\n        while t:\\n            if t % 3 == 2:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            t //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            s.add(x)\\n    return s\\n\\ndef _conflict_count(x: int, S: Set[int]) -> int:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Number of existing elements of S that would form a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP with x.\\"\\"\\"\\n    cnt = 0\\n    for y in S:\\n        if (x + y) & 1 == 0:               # possible midpoint\\n            mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n            if mid in S:\\n                cnt += 1\\n        # x as middle\\n        z = 2 * x - y\\n        if 1 <= z <= max(S) and z in S:\\n            cnt += 1\\n    return cnt\\n\\ndef _can_add(x: int, S: Set[int]) -> bool:\\n    \\"\\"\\"True iff adding x to S keeps the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free.\\"\\"\\"\\n    for y in S:\\n        if (x + y) & 1 == 0:\\n            mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n            if mid in S:\\n                return False\\n        z = 2 * x - y\\n        if 1 <= z <= max(S) and z in S:\\n            return False\\n    return True\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ---------- Phase 1: base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d set ----------\\n    S: Set[int] = _base3_no2(n)\\n\\n    # ---------- Phase 2: greedy insertion by conflict score ----------\\n    remaining = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n\\n    # compute conflict scores once; they change after each insertion,\\n    # so we keep a dynamic priority list.\\n    scores = {v: _conflict_count(v, S) for v in remaining}\\n    # sort ascending (fewest conflicts first)\\n    remaining.sort(key=lambda v: scores[v])\\n\\n    for x in remaining:\\n        if _can_add(x, S):\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # ---------- Phase 3: single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement ----------\\n    # Try to replace a conflicting element with a new one when it\\n    # eliminates exactly one conflict, thereby not shrinking the set.\\n    # Repeat until no more improvement.\\n    not_in_S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in_S)                       # deterministic randomness\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_in_S:\\n            # collect the elements of S that would conflict with x\\n            conflict_elems = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if (x + y) & 1 == 0:\\n                    mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if mid in S:\\n                        conflict_elems.append(y)\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                    conflict_elems.append(y)\\n                if len(conflict_elems) > 1:\\n                    break\\n            # if exactly one conflict, perform the swap\\n            if len(conflict_elems) == 1:\\n                y_remove = conflict_elems[0]\\n                S.remove(y_remove)\\n                S.add(x)\\n                # update helper lists\\n                not_in_S.remove(x)\\n                not_in_S.append(y_remove)\\n                improved = True\\n                break   # restart scan after a modification\\n\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code implementing new algorithm, with description in braces one sentence. Must not give extra explanations. Provide only code? Probably give description then code. Provide as a single response with description brace and then code block. Ensure deterministic and works for n=1094. Need to maximize size. Could use known construction: Salem-Spencer set via base-3 digits excluding 2 gives size about n^{log_2(??)} actually about n^{log_3 2}. For n=1094, size maybe ~... But we can improve via greedy addition as before. To get larger, maybe use random order with multiple passes of greedy then local search (hill climbing). Provide algorithm: start with base-3 set, then repeatedly try to insert remaining numbers in order of decreasing \\"conflict count\\" (numbers that cause few APs) and perform hill-climbing swaps of up to two removals. Keep deterministic using RNG seed.\\n\\nImplement can_add similar but also maintain adjacency map for speed maybe O(n*|S|). n small.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s produce new code with different parameters: use priority = count of existing elements that would conflict if added (lower first), compute once each iteration maybe.\\n\\nImplement iterative improvement: while possible add any remaining that can be added; after no addition, try to replace one element with two new ones (swap 1 out, 2 in) if net gain positive.\\n\\nSimplify: after greedy addition, try for each not-in-S candidate x, consider removing any conflicting y (maybe up to 2) and then see if after removal can add x plus maybe other not-in-S. For simplicity we can try single swap that increases size: find y such that removing y allows adding x and also some other z; but may be complex.\\n\\nGiven constraints, we can rely on greedy with random ordering seeded; maybe that\'s enough for large set ~?? Might not be maximal but acceptable.\\n\\nBut the prompt says \\"size of S should be as large as possible.\\" We can\'t guarantee optimal but provide heuristic.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement heuristic with two-phase: 1) base-3 set. 2) compute conflict score for each remaining number = number of triples it would create with current S (i.e., number of y where mid or other in S). Sort ascending. Then iterate adding if can. 3) local improvement: try to swap a single element y in S with a not-in-S element x if x can be added after removing y and does not reintroduce conflict; also try two-for-one if possible.\\n\\nImplement simple swap loop: for each x not in S, compute conflicts with S (list); if conflicts size ==1, replace that y with x.\\n\\nWill be similar to original but using priority ordering.\\n\\nProceed to write code.\\n\\n","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to output code implementing new algorithm, with description in braces one sentence. Must not give extra explanations. Provide only code? Probably give description then code. Provide as a single response with description brace and then code block. Ensure deterministic and works for n=1094. Need to maximize size. Could use known construction: Salem-Spencer set via base-3 digits excluding 2 gives size about n^{log_2(??)} actually about n^{log_3 2}. For n=1094, size maybe ~... But we can improve via greedy addition as before. To get larger, maybe use random order with multiple passes of greedy then local search (hill climbing). Provide algorithm: start with base-3 set, then repeatedly try to insert remaining numbers in order of decreasing \\"conflict count\\" (numbers that cause few APs) and perform hill-climbing swaps of up to two removals. Keep deterministic using RNG seed.\\n\\nImplement can_add similar but also maintain adjacency map for speed maybe O(n*|S|). n small.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s produce new code with different parameters: use priority = count of existing elements that would conflict if added (lower first), compute once each iteration maybe.\\n\\nImplement iterative improvement: while possible add any remaining that can be added; after no addition, try to replace one element with two new ones (swap 1 out, 2 in) if net gain positive.\\n\\nSimplify: after greedy addition, try for each not-in-S candidate x, consider removing any conflicting y (maybe up to 2) and then see if after removal can add x plus maybe other not-in-S. For simplicity we can try single swap that increases size: find y such that removing y allows adding x and also some other z; but may be complex.\\n\\nGiven constraints, we can rely on greedy with random ordering seeded; maybe that\'s enough for large set ~?? Might not be maximal but acceptable.\\n\\nBut the prompt says \\"size of S should be as large as possible.\\" We can\'t guarantee optimal but provide heuristic.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement heuristic with two-phase: 1) base-3 set. 2) compute conflict score for each remaining number = number of triples it would create with current S (i.e., number of y where mid or other in S). Sort ascending. Then iterate adding if can. 3) local improvement: try to swap a single element y in S with a not-in-S element x if x can be added after removing y and does not reintroduce conflict; also try two-for-one if possible.\\n\\nImplement simple swap loop: for each x not in S, compute conflicts with S (list); if conflicts size ==1, replace that y with x.\\n\\nWill be similar to original but using priority ordering.\\n\\nProceed to write code.\\n\\n"}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1513,"prompt_tokens":1595,"total_tokens":3108}}'
