res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-3b659a100436bdf7e54cc697514935b7","created":1766037123,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A greedy construction that orders candidates by a mixed \xe2\x80\x9cmiddle\xe2\x80\x91vs\xe2\x80\x91endpoint\xe2\x80\x9d conflict score (favoring numbers that are rarely midpoints of 3\xe2\x80\x91APs) and then performs a deterministic single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Union\\n\\ndef _make_rng(rng: Union[None, int, np.random.Generator]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from None / int / Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        rng = 0\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Build a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (intended for n=1094).\\n\\n    Parameters\\n    ----------\\n    n : int\\n        Upper bound of the universe (inclusive).\\n    rng : None, int or np.random.Generator, optional\\n        Random seed / generator for deterministic tie\xe2\x80\x91breaking.\\n\\n    Returns\\n    -------\\n    List[int]\\n        Sorted list S \xe2\x8a\x86 {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} that contains no three\xe2\x80\x91term arithmetic progression.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _make_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1.  Compute a mixed score for each element.\\n    #     * middle_cnt  = # of triples where the element is the middle point.\\n    #                       = min(x\xe2\x80\x911, n\xe2\x80\x91x)\\n    #     * endpoint_cnt = # of triples where the element is an endpoint.\\n    #                       = (x\xe2\x80\x911)//2  +  (n\xe2\x80\x91x)//2\\n    #     * score = 3*middle_cnt + endpoint_cnt   (larger \xe2\x86\x92 more risky)\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    scores = np.empty(n + 1, dtype=int)\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        middle_cnt = min(x - 1, n - x)\\n        endpoint_cnt = (x - 1) // 2 + (n - x) // 2\\n        scores[x] = 3 * middle_cnt + endpoint_cnt\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2.  Order candidates by (score, random tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker).\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    candidates = np.arange(1, n + 1)\\n    gen.shuffle(candidates)                     # random tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker\\n    # stable sort by score (ascending)\\n    candidates = candidates[np.argsort(scores[candidates], kind=\'stable\')]\\n\\n    present = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=bool)\\n    S = []          # list of selected elements (unsorted)\\n\\n    # fast check whether inserting x creates a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\\n    def can_insert(x: int) -> bool:\\n        # x with any y already in S must not create a middle that is present\\n        for y in S:\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:               # integer midpoint possible\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[mid]:\\n                    return False\\n            # x could be the middle of (y, z)\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # greedy insertion\\n    for x in candidates:\\n        if can_insert(int(x)):\\n            present[int(x)] = True\\n            S.append(int(x))\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3.  Single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement.\\n    #     Replace one element of S by a non\xe2\x80\x91selected element that conflicts\\n    #     with exactly one current element, thereby keeping the size unchanged\\n    #     but possibly freeing room for later insertions.\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    not_present = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_present)\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_present:\\n            conflicts = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                # check if adding x would clash with y\\n                if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                    mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if present[mid]:\\n                        conflicts.append(y)\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n                if len(conflicts) > 1:\\n                    break\\n            if len(conflicts) == 1:\\n                # perform swap\\n                rem = conflicts[0]\\n                present[rem] = False\\n                S.remove(rem)\\n\\n                present[x] = True\\n                S.append(x)\\n\\n                not_present.remove(x)\\n                not_present.append(rem)\\n\\n                improved = True\\n                break   # restart scan after a successful swap\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4.  Final pass: try to insert any remaining numbers (they may now fit).\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    leftovers = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(leftovers)\\n    for x in leftovers:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n            S.append(x)\\n\\n    S.sort()\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"The user wants a new algorithm with different parameter settings of the score function. Must output just code, with maybe braces description. Provide one-sentence description inside braces. Implement heuristic function.\\n\\nWe need to design a new score function: perhaps use weight based on binary representation or modular residues to prioritize numbers that are powers of 2 or numbers with high odd part? Could use \\"2-adic valuation\\": numbers with many factors of 2 are less likely to be middle of AP? Actually middle of AP is arithmetic average of two numbers => if numbers are both odd or both even, middle integer may be integer. The classic Salem-Spencer set can be constructed via base-3 representation without digit \'1\' (like Cantor set) yields size about n^{log_2 3}? Actually known construction: take numbers whose base-3 representation uses only digits 0 and 2, yields set of size approx n^{log_3 2} ~ n^{0.6309}. For n=1094, that\'s maybe ~something 1100^{0.63} ~ about 1100^0.63 = exp(0.63*ln1100)=exp(0.63*7.0)=exp(4.41)=82. So about 80 elements. We can aim for more using greedy.\\n\\nWe can design score as number of APs where x is middle element only (since being middle is more restrictive). Or count triples where x is endpoint only. So we can set weight = endpointCount - middleCount or something.\\n\\nImplement: for each x compute count_endpoints where x is endpoint: count of y such that (x+y) even and mid in range. That\'s essentially number of potential mids. Similarly middleCount = number of pairs (a,c) with a<c and (a+c)/2 = x => a + c = 2x => for each a < x, c = 2x - a, check c <= n. That\'s same as number of a values such that 1<=2x-a <= n -> a between 2x-n and x-1. So middleCount = min(x-1, n - x). Could compute directly.\\n\\nWe\'ll use score = middleCount (more restrictive) so we prefer low middleCount (i.e., near edges). That\'s similar to original.\\n\\nBut they ask \\"different parameter settings of the score function provided\\". So we could weight endpointCount heavily.\\n\\nImplement: endpointCount for x: number of y > x such that (x+y) even and midpoint exists (automatically) i.e., count of y where (x+y) even and midpoint <= n (always). That\'s roughly about (n - x)/2.\\n\\nMaybe we just compute score = middleCount * 2 + endpointCount. Or could compute score = middleCount - endpointCount.\\n\\nWe\'ll create algorithm:\\n\\n1. Compute middleCount = min(x-1, n-x).\\n2. Compute endpointCount = ((n - x) // 2) (approx) plus (x-1)//2 for y < x? Actually endpoints can be both sides; but we only need count of other endpoint where x participates, whichever endpoint side. total endpointCount = ( (x-1)//2 + (n - x)//2 ) maybe.\\n\\nSimplify: endpointCount = (x-1)//2 + (n - x)//2.\\n\\nScore = middleCount*3 + endpointCount (higher score = more risky). Then order by ascending score.\\n\\nImplement greedy insertion in that order.\\n\\nLocal improvement: try to replace one element with two others (increase size) maybe. Simpler: try single swap similar to original but using different conflict detection.\\n\\nWill produce decent set.\\n\\nMake deterministic using rng.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s code. Ensure can_insert function fast using boolean array.\\n\\nImplement conflict detection: same as earlier.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nWill produce set maybe up to 84.\\n\\nLet\'s produce final answer.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"The user wants a new algorithm with different parameter settings of the score function. Must output just code, with maybe braces description. Provide one-sentence description inside braces. Implement heuristic function.\\n\\nWe need to design a new score function: perhaps use weight based on binary representation or modular residues to prioritize numbers that are powers of 2 or numbers with high odd part? Could use \\"2-adic valuation\\": numbers with many factors of 2 are less likely to be middle of AP? Actually middle of AP is arithmetic average of two numbers => if numbers are both odd or both even, middle integer may be integer. The classic Salem-Spencer set can be constructed via base-3 representation without digit \'1\' (like Cantor set) yields size about n^{log_2 3}? Actually known construction: take numbers whose base-3 representation uses only digits 0 and 2, yields set of size approx n^{log_3 2} ~ n^{0.6309}. For n=1094, that\'s maybe ~something 1100^{0.63} ~ about 1100^0.63 = exp(0.63*ln1100)=exp(0.63*7.0)=exp(4.41)=82. So about 80 elements. We can aim for more using greedy.\\n\\nWe can design score as number of APs where x is middle element only (since being middle is more restrictive). Or count triples where x is endpoint only. So we can set weight = endpointCount - middleCount or something.\\n\\nImplement: for each x compute count_endpoints where x is endpoint: count of y such that (x+y) even and mid in range. That\'s essentially number of potential mids. Similarly middleCount = number of pairs (a,c) with a<c and (a+c)/2 = x => a + c = 2x => for each a < x, c = 2x - a, check c <= n. That\'s same as number of a values such that 1<=2x-a <= n -> a between 2x-n and x-1. So middleCount = min(x-1, n - x). Could compute directly.\\n\\nWe\'ll use score = middleCount (more restrictive) so we prefer low middleCount (i.e., near edges). That\'s similar to original.\\n\\nBut they ask \\"different parameter settings of the score function provided\\". So we could weight endpointCount heavily.\\n\\nImplement: endpointCount for x: number of y > x such that (x+y) even and midpoint exists (automatically) i.e., count of y where (x+y) even and midpoint <= n (always). That\'s roughly about (n - x)/2.\\n\\nMaybe we just compute score = middleCount * 2 + endpointCount. Or could compute score = middleCount - endpointCount.\\n\\nWe\'ll create algorithm:\\n\\n1. Compute middleCount = min(x-1, n-x).\\n2. Compute endpointCount = ((n - x) // 2) (approx) plus (x-1)//2 for y < x? Actually endpoints can be both sides; but we only need count of other endpoint where x participates, whichever endpoint side. total endpointCount = ( (x-1)//2 + (n - x)//2 ) maybe.\\n\\nSimplify: endpointCount = (x-1)//2 + (n - x)//2.\\n\\nScore = middleCount*3 + endpointCount (higher score = more risky). Then order by ascending score.\\n\\nImplement greedy insertion in that order.\\n\\nLocal improvement: try to replace one element with two others (increase size) maybe. Simpler: try single swap similar to original but using different conflict detection.\\n\\nWill produce decent set.\\n\\nMake deterministic using rng.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s code. Ensure can_insert function fast using boolean array.\\n\\nImplement conflict detection: same as earlier.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nWill produce set maybe up to 84.\\n\\nLet\'s produce final answer."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1914,"prompt_tokens":1815,"total_tokens":3729}}' Obj:  -127.0| Obj:  -128.0|
