res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-8c0937cf6cdba04c4b14bbfdcd17b30c","created":1766037137,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A priority\xe2\x80\x91driven greedy construction that repeatedly removes a randomly chosen element from the current set and re\xe2\x80\x91greedily refills the \xe2\x80\x9chole\xe2\x80\x9d with higher\xe2\x80\x91priority numbers, keeping any improvement.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Union\\n\\ndef _make_rng(rng: Union[None, int, np.random.Generator]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from a seed, a Generator, or None.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        rng = 0\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Build a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (intended for n=1094).\\n\\n    The algorithm works in two phases:\\n      1. Greedy insertion following a random priority order.\\n      2. A bounded local\xe2\x80\x91search that repeatedly removes one element,\\n         then greedily re\xe2\x80\x91inserts the best reachable elements.\\n    The result is deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _make_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1.  Random priority for each integer (deterministic via rng)\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    priority = gen.random(n + 1)         # index 0 unused; values in [0,1)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2.  Greedy insertion respecting 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free condition\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    present = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=bool)   # present[i] == True  \xe2\x87\x94 i \xe2\x88\x88 S\\n    S = []                                   # list of members for fast loops\\n\\n    def can_insert(x: int) -> bool:\\n        \\"\\"\\"Return True iff x can be added without creating a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\"\\"\\"\\n        # check against all current elements in S\\n        for y in S:\\n            # x and y as endpoints \xe2\x86\x92 midpoint must not be present\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[mid]:\\n                    return False\\n            # x as middle, y as endpoint\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # order candidates by increasing priority (ties broken by value)\\n    candidates = list(range(1, n + 1))\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda v: (priority[v], v))\\n\\n    for v in candidates:\\n        if can_insert(v):\\n            present[v] = True\\n            S.append(v)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3.  Small local search: remove one element, try to refill.\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    max_iters = 1500                     # enough for n\xe2\x89\x881100, still fast\\n    for _ in range(max_iters):\\n        if not S:          # nothing to remove\\n            break\\n        # pick an element to temporarily discard (deterministic choice)\\n        rm = gen.choice(S)\\n        present[rm] = False\\n        S.remove(rm)\\n\\n        # recompute a fresh list of candidates that are currently absent\\n        absent = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n        # sort by the original priority (higher priority first -> lower value)\\n        absent.sort(key=lambda v: (priority[v], v))\\n\\n        added = []\\n        for v in absent:\\n            if can_insert(v):\\n                present[v] = True\\n                added.append(v)\\n\\n        # net gain?\\n        if len(added) > 1:                     # we removed 1, added >1 \xe2\x86\x92 improvement\\n            S.extend(added)\\n            # keep changes, continue\\n            continue\\n        else:\\n            # revert removal (undo any insertions made in this attempt)\\n            for v in added:\\n                present[v] = False\\n            present[rm] = True\\n            S.append(rm)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4.  Return sorted result for reproducibility\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    S.sort()\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to produce new algorithm, different from given ones. Provide description in braces single sentence. Implement heuristic function. Should aim to find large Salem-Spencer set for n=1094. Could use known construction: use Behrend\'s dense construction? For small n, we can do greedy with random ordering, plus simulated annealing, but need deterministic.\\n\\nWe must produce algorithm different from both: first uses conflict score and single-swap local improvement. Second starts with base-3 \\"no-2\\" set. So we need a new approach: maybe use binary representation and keep numbers with a limited number of ones; like set of numbers congruent to 1 mod 3? That\'s similar though. Could use greedy with ordering by modulo classes derived from a Sidon set, or use BFS to find large independent set in 3-AP hypergraph via heuristic like max independent set using random priority (Greedy Independent Set). That\'s similar but could be considered different.\\n\\nWe can use a \\"randomized priority order\\" based on a hash of each number using RNG, then greedy add, then attempt local improvements via removing a small subset and re-running greedy (local search). That is distinct.\\n\\nImplementation: compute a random priority for each element via rng.integers, sort by priority. Greedy add when no AP formed. Then perform iterative \\"delete a random element from S and try to re-add many others\\" to improve size, limited iterations.\\n\\nMake deterministic via rng.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nLet\'s code.\\n\\nNeed helper can_insert as before but fast using boolean array. Use numpy bool array present.\\n\\nImplement heuristic:\\n\\n- rng = make rng.\\n\\n- assign priority = rng.random(n)+... trivial.\\n\\n- order candidates by priority.\\n\\n- greedy to build S.\\n\\n- local improvement: for iter in range(max_iter=2000 maybe) :\\n\\n   - pick a random element from S (using rng.choice) to remove temporarily.\\n\\n   - compute set of candidates = numbers not present that become insertable now (including removed element). Run greedy over those candidates sorted by same priority (or fresh) to try to add more than one element.\\n\\n   - If net size increased, keep changes; else revert removal.\\n\\nBecause n small, we can do few iterations.\\n\\nWe need deterministic same rng will produce same sequence. Use rng.permutation to generate order.\\n\\nOk implement.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s write.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to produce new algorithm, different from given ones. Provide description in braces single sentence. Implement heuristic function. Should aim to find large Salem-Spencer set for n=1094. Could use known construction: use Behrend\'s dense construction? For small n, we can do greedy with random ordering, plus simulated annealing, but need deterministic.\\n\\nWe must produce algorithm different from both: first uses conflict score and single-swap local improvement. Second starts with base-3 \\"no-2\\" set. So we need a new approach: maybe use binary representation and keep numbers with a limited number of ones; like set of numbers congruent to 1 mod 3? That\'s similar though. Could use greedy with ordering by modulo classes derived from a Sidon set, or use BFS to find large independent set in 3-AP hypergraph via heuristic like max independent set using random priority (Greedy Independent Set). That\'s similar but could be considered different.\\n\\nWe can use a \\"randomized priority order\\" based on a hash of each number using RNG, then greedy add, then attempt local improvements via removing a small subset and re-running greedy (local search). That is distinct.\\n\\nImplementation: compute a random priority for each element via rng.integers, sort by priority. Greedy add when no AP formed. Then perform iterative \\"delete a random element from S and try to re-add many others\\" to improve size, limited iterations.\\n\\nMake deterministic via rng.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nLet\'s code.\\n\\nNeed helper can_insert as before but fast using boolean array. Use numpy bool array present.\\n\\nImplement heuristic:\\n\\n- rng = make rng.\\n\\n- assign priority = rng.random(n)+... trivial.\\n\\n- order candidates by priority.\\n\\n- greedy to build S.\\n\\n- local improvement: for iter in range(max_iter=2000 maybe) :\\n\\n   - pick a random element from S (using rng.choice) to remove temporarily.\\n\\n   - compute set of candidates = numbers not present that become insertable now (including removed element). Run greedy over those candidates sorted by same priority (or fresh) to try to add more than one element.\\n\\n   - If net size increased, keep changes; else revert removal.\\n\\nBecause n small, we can do few iterations.\\n\\nWe need deterministic same rng will produce same sequence. Use rng.permutation to generate order.\\n\\nOk implement.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s write."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1370,"prompt_tokens":2841,"total_tokens":4211}}'
