res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-128a0b8aff01866918d91f6163afdc89","created":1766037227,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"**Common backbone idea:** both given algorithms greedily build a set based on a static priority (conflict score) and then perform a limited single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement.\\n\\n{New algorithm: start from the full universe, repeatedly delete the element that participates in the largest number of remaining 3\xe2\x80\x91APs (breaking ties deterministically with the provided RNG) until no 3\xe2\x80\x91AP remains, then greedily re\xe2\x80\x91insert any removable numbers.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Union\\n\\ndef _make_rng(rng: Union[None, int, np.random.Generator]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        rng = 0\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Construct a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (n is expected to be 1094).\\n    Deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _make_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ---------- 1. enumerate all 3\xe2\x80\x91AP triples ----------\\n    triples = []                     # list of (a,b,c) with a<b<c\\n    elem_triples = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]   # indices of triples containing each element\\n    for a in range(1, n + 1):\\n        for b in range(a + 1, n + 1):\\n            c = 2 * b - a\\n            if c > n:\\n                continue\\n            triples.append((a, b, c))\\n            idx = len(triples) - 1\\n            elem_triples[a].append(idx)\\n            elem_triples[b].append(idx)\\n            elem_triples[c].append(idx)\\n\\n    m = len(triples)\\n\\n    # ---------- 2. initialise present set ----------\\n    present = np.ones(n + 1, dtype=bool)   # index 0 unused, start with all elements\\n    present[0] = False\\n\\n    # ---------- 3. conflict counters ----------\\n    conflict_cnt = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=int)\\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\\n        conflict_cnt[i] = len(elem_triples[i])\\n\\n    # ---------- 4. deterministic tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker order ----------\\n    order = np.arange(n + 1)\\n    gen.shuffle(order[1:])               # shuffle only 1..n\\n    order_rank = np.empty(n + 1, dtype=int)\\n    order_rank[order] = np.arange(n + 1)\\n\\n    # ---------- 5. iterative removal ----------\\n    # keep a set of active triples (still fully present)\\n    active = np.ones(m, dtype=bool)\\n\\n    while True:\\n        # find element with maximal conflict count among those still present\\n        max_cnt = 0\\n        cand = -1\\n        for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n            if not present[x]:\\n                continue\\n            cnt = conflict_cnt[x]\\n            if cnt > max_cnt or (cnt == max_cnt and cnt > 0 and order_rank[x] < order_rank[cand]):\\n                max_cnt = cnt\\n                cand = x\\n        if max_cnt == 0:\\n            break   # no remaining APs\\n\\n        # remove the chosen element\\n        present[cand] = False\\n        # deactivate all triples that contain cand\\n        for ti in elem_triples[cand]:\\n            if not active[ti]:\\n                continue\\n            active[ti] = False\\n            a, b, c = triples[ti]\\n            for v in (a, b, c):\\n                if v != cand:\\n                    conflict_cnt[v] -= 1\\n\\n    # ---------- 6. final greedy insertion of leftovers ----------\\n    # leftover = numbers not present\\n    leftovers = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(leftovers)\\n\\n    # helper to test insertion\\n    def can_insert(x: int) -> bool:\\n        for y in range(1, n + 1):\\n            if not present[y]:\\n                continue\\n            # x and y as endpoints\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[mid]:\\n                    return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    for x in leftovers:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n\\n    S = [i for i in range(1, n + 1) if present[i]]\\n    S.sort()\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code only, no extra explanations. Must first identify common backbone idea, then describe new algorithm in one sentence inside braces, then implement. Must not give extra text beyond that? The instruction: \\"Please help me create a new algorithm ... Firstly, identify the common backbone idea... Secondly, based on the backbone idea describe your new algorithm in one sentence. The description must be inside a brace. Thirdly, implement it in Python...\\" And \\"Do not give additional explanations.\\" So output should be: first a line identifying common backbone idea, then a brace with description, then the code.\\n\\nThus format: maybe something like:\\n\\nCommon backbone: greedy selection based on a static priority order plus local swap improvement.\\n\\n{New algorithm: ...}\\n\\nThen code.\\n\\nMake sure deterministic.\\n\\nWe need new algorithm totally different form but motivated. Perhaps use a random greedy + iterative deepening, or use construction from known Salem-Spencer sets: base-3 representation? For n=1094, we can use the classic construction: take numbers whose base-3 representation contains only digits 0 and 1 (no 2). This yields a 3-AP-free set (since it\'s a subset of a Sidon set?). Actually known: the set of numbers with no digit 2 in base 3 is progression-free (Stanley). That\'s the Cantor set. For n up to 3^k -1, that gives size 2^k. For n=1094, compute max k such that 3^k -1 \xe2\x89\xa4 1094. 3^6=729, 3^7=2187 >1094, so up to 3^6-1=728. That set size 2^6=64. That\'s far from optimum. We need larger.\\n\\nMaybe use a greedy random algorithm with backtracking (like keep an independent set in a hypergraph). Could use simulated annealing or iterative removal of offending triples.\\n\\nWe could implement: start with all numbers, while there exists a 3-AP, remove the middle element of a randomly chosen AP (or the one with highest conflict score). That\'s different from given which starts empty and adds. This uses removal approach.\\n\\nLet\'s design: compute all AP triples in the range. Maintain present array True. While any AP present, pick an AP (a,b,c) where all present. Choose removal candidate based on conflict score (precomputed) or randomly with seed. Remove that element and update. After no APs, we have AP-free set. Then try local add of leftover numbers that can be added.\\n\\nThat\'s different form: start full and prune.\\n\\nImplement efficiently: precompute list of triples maybe O(n^2). n=1094, n^2 ~1.2M, feasible.\\n\\nWe\'ll compute for each middle b all endpoints a,c: for each a<b, c=2*b - a if <=n. That\'s O(n^2). Store triples as list.\\n\\nLoop: while True, find any triple where present[a] and present[b] and present[c]. We can maintain a queue of triples that are currently alive. Initially add all triples. Then each removal may invalidate some triples; we can just recompute naive each iteration maybe okay because set shrinks quickly. Simpler: loop scanning all triples each iteration O(num_triples * removals) could be heavy but n small.\\n\\nAlternative: use conflict score: for each element count number of current triples it participates in. Choose element with highest count to remove.\\n\\nImplementation: compute adjacency list of triples per element. Maintain counts. While any element has count>0, pick element with max count, remove it, update counts of other elements in those triples (decrement). Use a priority queue (max-heap) but need updates; can recompute max each loo