res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-feb81ff403228d0158f8d1c062ce759b","created":1766037243,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A deterministic greedy\xe2\x80\x91plus\xe2\x80\x91limited\xe2\x80\x91swap construction that starts from the base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d Salem\xe2\x80\x91Spencer set, iteratively inserts conflict\xe2\x80\x91free numbers ordered by fewest induced violations, then performs bounded single\xe2\x80\x91element swaps to unblock further insertions.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _rng_from(seed):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a numpy Generator from None, int, array\xe2\x80\x91like or Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(seed, np.random.Generator):\\n        return seed\\n    if seed is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Returns a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\n    The algorithm is deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _rng_from(rng)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1. Base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d set \xe2\x80\x93 guaranteed AP\xe2\x80\x91free\\n    base = set()\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        y = x\\n        ok = True\\n        while y:\\n            if y % 3 == 2:            # digit 2 \xe2\x86\x92 discard\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            y //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            base.add(x)\\n\\n    S = set(base)                     # current solution\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # Helper: list of existing elements that would clash with candidate x\\n    def conflicts(x):\\n        \\"\\"\\"Return a list of elements in S that would create a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP with x.\\"\\"\\"\\n        bad = []\\n        for y in S:\\n            # x and y as opposite ends\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:          # integer midpoint\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if mid in S:\\n                    bad.append(y)\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                bad.append(y)\\n        # deduplicate\\n        return list(dict.fromkeys(bad))\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2. Greedy insertion ordered by fewest conflicts\\n    remaining = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    # initial random shuffle for deterministic tie\xe2\x80\x91breaking\\n    gen.shuffle(remaining)\\n\\n    # compute conflict counts once\\n    conflict_counts = {x: len(conflicts(x)) for x in remaining}\\n    # sort by count (fewest first), then by the shuffled order\\n    remaining.sort(key=lambda x: (conflict_counts[x], remaining.index(x)))\\n\\n    for x in remaining:\\n        if not conflicts(x):               # zero conflicts \xe2\x86\x92 safe to add\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3. Limited single\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement\\n    #    Try to replace a blocking element with a non\xe2\x80\x91member if it does\\n    #    not shrink the set; repeat a bounded number of passes.\\n    not_in = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in)\\n\\n    max_passes = 5\\n    for _ in range(max_passes):\\n        made_change = False\\n        for x in not_in:\\n            bad = conflicts(x)\\n            if len(bad) == 1:               # only one element blocks x\\n                y = bad[0]\\n                # perform the swap\\n                S.remove(y)\\n                S.add(x)\\n                not_in.remove(x)\\n                not_in.append(y)\\n                made_change = True\\n                break                       # restart scan after a change\\n        if not made_change:\\n            break\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4. Final pass: try to add any newly unblocked numbers\\n    added = True\\n    while added:\\n        added = False\\n        candidates = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n        gen.shuffle(candidates)\\n        for x in candidates:\\n            if not conflicts(x):\\n                S.add(x)\\n                added = True\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code only, after a one-sentence description within braces. Must create a new algorithm different form but can be modification. Must aim to find large set for n=1094. Provide deterministic with rng. Return list. Provide description in braces first line. No extra explanations. Ensure code runs.\\n\\nWe can implement approach: start with greedy using ordering by residue class modulo small base (e.g., using van der Waerden block construction). Perhaps we use a known construction: take numbers whose base-3 representation avoids digit \'2\' (same as before) then apply addition of numbers that are far apart using a priority based on number of 3-AP conflicts (score). Use a simple simulated annealing style local search for limited time.\\n\\nBut must be different from given algorithm. Provide new algorithm: start with empty set, iterate numbers sorted by a score (like number of potential conflicts if added). Use heuristic: compute for each candidate the number of pairs (y) such that midpoint or opposite would be in set; choose candidate with minimal conflicts. Use deterministic order via rng tie-breaking. Then add if no conflict. After initial greedy, perform limited swapping: try to replace any element with a non-member that yields larger set by exploring removal of up to two conflicting members and adding new ones, using deterministic RNG.\\n\\nImplement.\\n\\nWill need functions: can_add, conflict_count.\\n\\nComplexities O(n^2) maybe okay for 1094.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s craft code.\\n\\nNeed rng handling similar.\\n\\nImplement heuristic:\\n\\n- Convert rng.\\n\\n- Compute initial order: list 1..n shuffled.\\n\\n- Initialize empty set S.\\n\\n- While candidates remain: for each candidate compute number of existing elements that would create AP if added (conflicts). Pick candidate with minimum conflicts (0 first). If conflicts ==0, add; else keep for later.\\n\\nSimplify: loop through shuffled list, add if can_add.\\n\\nThat\'s similar to given though. Need different.\\n\\nWe could use a BFS-like approach: build using greedy on residues modulo 2 and 3: choose numbers where binary representation has even number of 1s? Not sure.\\n\\nBetter to implement a simple backtracking with limited depth: start with base-3 no-2 set (size maybe ~ (2/3)n). Then try to add numbers in decreasing order of distance from existing set, verifying. That\'s similar.\\n\\nWe just need to output anything valid, not necessarily optimal, but goal to maximize. But they may test size? Probably they want better than simple base-3? Not sure.\\n\\nWe can attempt to compute exact maximal Salem-Spencer set for n=1094 by using integer programming? Could be heavy. But we can approximate with greedy plus local improvement.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n1. Build initial set via base-3 no-2.\\n\\n2. Create list of remaining numbers sorted by descending number of 3-AP constraints (i.e., number of pairs (a,b) in S that would conflict if added). That is compute conflict count. Then attempt to add those with zero conflict, then after one pass, recompute scores for remaining and try again.\\n\\n3. After no more additions, perform local search: for each not-in, try to swap out up to two members that are conflicts to add it, gaining net +1 if possible (i.e., remove k elements and add 1 -> net maybe 0 but can open further adds). We can allow removal of up to 2 and then re-run greedy.\\n\\nSimplify: implement hill climbing: random order of not-in; if can replace a conflicting element (single) with candidate that keeps set size same and may allow more additions later, do it. Then after swaps, run greedy addition again.\\n\\nWill be deterministic with rng.\\n\\nImplement functions: conflicts_of(x, S) returns list of elements in S that would form AP with x (as endpoint or middle). Use similar loop.\\n\\nImplement can_add.\\n\\nImplement attempt_swap: if len(conflicts)==1, replace.\\n\\nThen after swaps, add again.\\n\\nOk produce code.\\n\\nMake sure to return list sorted.\\n\\nLet\'s craft final answer with description.\\n\\n","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to output code only, after a one-sentence description within braces. Must create a new algorithm different form but can be modification. Must aim to find large set for n=1094. Provide deterministic with rng. Return list. Provide description in braces first line. No extra explanations. Ensure code runs.\\n\\nWe can implement approach: start with greedy using ordering by residue class modulo small base (e.g., using van der Waerden block construction). Perhaps we use a known construction: take numbers whose base-3 representation avoids digit \'2\' (same as before) then apply addition of numbers that are far apart using a priority based on number of 3-AP conflicts (score). Use a simple simulated annealing style local search for limited time.\\n\\nBut must be different from given algorithm. Provide new algorithm: start with empty set, iterate numbers sorted by a score (like number of potential conflicts if added). Use heuristic: compute for each candidate the number of pairs (y) such that midpoint or opposite would be in set; choose candidate with minimal conflicts. Use deterministic order via rng tie-breaking. Then add if no conflict. After initial greedy, perform limited swapping: try to replace any element with a non-member that yields larger set by exploring removal of up to two conflicting members and adding new ones, using deterministic RNG.\\n\\nImplement.\\n\\nWill need functions: can_add, conflict_count.\\n\\nComplexities O(n^2) maybe okay for 1094.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nLet\'s craft code.\\n\\nNeed rng handling similar.\\n\\nImplement heuristic:\\n\\n- Convert rng.\\n\\n- Compute initial order: list 1..n shuffled.\\n\\n- Initialize empty set S.\\n\\n- While candidates remain: for each candidate compute number of existing elements that would create AP if added (conflicts). Pick candidate with minimum conflicts (0 first). If conflicts ==0, add; else keep for later.\\n\\nSimplify: loop through shuffled list, add if can_add.\\n\\nThat\'s similar to given though. Need different.\\n\\nWe could use a BFS-like approach: build using greedy on residues modulo 2 and 3: choose numbers where binary representation has even number of 1s? Not sure.\\n\\nBetter to implement a simple backtracking with limited depth: start with base-3 no-2 set (size maybe ~ (2/3)n). Then try to add numbers in decreasing order of distance from existing set, verifying. That\'s similar.\\n\\nWe just need to output anything valid, not necessarily optimal, but goal to maximize. But they may test size? Probably they want better than simple base-3? Not sure.\\n\\nWe can attempt to compute exact maximal Salem-Spencer set for n=1094 by using integer programming? Could be heavy. But we can approximate with greedy plus local improvement.\\n\\nWe\'ll implement:\\n\\n1. Build initial set via base-3 no-2.\\n\\n2. Create list of remaining numbers sorted by descending number of 3-AP constraints (i.e., number of pairs (a,b) in S that would conflict if added). That is compute conflict count. Then attempt to add those with zero conflict, then after one pass, recompute scores for remaining and try again.\\n\\n3. After no more additions, perform local search: for each not-in, try to swap out up to two members that are conflicts to add it, gaining net +1 if possible (i.e., remove k elements and add 1 -> net maybe 0 but can open further adds). We can allow removal of up to 2 and then re-run greedy.\\n\\nSimplify: implement hill climbing: random order of not-in; if can replace a conflicting element (single) with candidate that keeps set size same and may allow more additions later, do it. Then after swaps, run greedy addition again.\\n\\nWill be deterministic with rng.\\n\\nImplement functions: conflicts_of(x, S) returns list of elements in S that would form AP with x (as endpoint or middle). Use similar loop.\\n\\nImplement can_add.\\n\\nImplement attempt_swap: if len(conflicts)==1, replace.\\n\\nThen after swaps, add again.\\n\\nOk produce code.\\n\\nMake sure to return list sorted.\\n\\nLet\'s craft final answer with description.\\n\\n"}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1722,"prompt_tokens":1593,"total_tokens":3315}}'
