res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-15b7f850715730ac28a5913076eb6736","created":1766037227,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"**Backbone idea in the two supplied heuristics**  \\nBoth start from the guaranteed 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x91in\xe2\x80\x91base\xe2\x80\x913\xe2\x80\x9d set, then greedily scan the remaining numbers in a (pseudo\xe2\x80\x91random) order, adding any element that does not create a three\xe2\x80\x91term progression, followed by a tiny local\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement.\\n\\n**New algorithm (one\xe2\x80\x91sentence description)**  \\n{Process the numbers in decreasing order of their binary\xe2\x80\x91weight (popcount), breaking ties with a deterministic RNG; greedily insert any element that keeps the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free, then perform a bounded \xe2\x80\x9cswap\xe2\x80\x91or\xe2\x80\x91replace\xe2\x80\x9d hill\xe2\x80\x91climb that tries to exchange a single conflicting member for a currently\xe2\x80\x91unselected element whenever the swap does not reduce the set size.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a NumPy Generator from various rng specifications.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef heuristic(n, rng=None):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Return a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (n will be 1094).\\n    The algorithm follows the description given above and is deterministic\\n    for a fixed (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # ---------- 1. candidate ordering ----------\\n    # weight = number of 1\xe2\x80\x91bits; larger weight first.\\n    candidates = list(range(1, n + 1))\\n    weights = np.array([bin(x).count(\'1\') for x in candidates], dtype=int)\\n    # shuffle inside each weight class for determinism\\n    order = []\\n    for w in sorted(set(weights), reverse=True):\\n        bucket = [c for c, wt in zip(candidates, weights) if wt == w]\\n        gen.shuffle(bucket)\\n        order.extend(bucket)\\n\\n    # ---------- 2. fast AP\xe2\x80\x91free test ----------\\n    S = set()\\n    # auxiliary dict mapping each value to the set of mids that would be formed with it\\n    # not needed; just O(|S|) scan because n is small.\\n    def can_add(x):\\n        \\"\\"\\"True iff x can be added to S without creating a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\"\\"\\"\\n        for y in S:\\n            s = x + y\\n            if s & 1:          # odd \xe2\x86\x92 no integer middle\\n                continue\\n            mid = s >> 1\\n            if mid in S:\\n                return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # ---------- 3. greedy insertion ----------\\n    for x in order:\\n        if can_add(x):\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # ---------- 4. bounded hill\xe2\x80\x91climb (swap/replace) ----------\\n    not_in = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in)\\n\\n    # limit number of attempts to keep runtime low\\n    max_passes = 5\\n    for _ in range(max_passes):\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_in:\\n            # collect the elements of S that conflict with x\\n            conflicts = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if (x + y) & 1 == 0:\\n                    mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if mid in S:\\n                        conflicts.append(y)\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n                if len(conflicts) > 2:\\n                    break\\n            # if at most one element blocks x, we can swap without shrinking\\n            if len(conflicts) <= 1:\\n                if conflicts:\\n                    S.remove(conflicts[0])\\n                S.add(x)\\n                not_in.remove(x)\\n                if conflicts:\\n                    not_in.append(conflicts[0])\\n                improved = True\\n                break   # restart scanning after any change\\n        if not improved:\\n            break\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to produce answer: identify common backbone idea, describe new algorithm in one sentence inside braces, then provide code implementation. Must not give extra explanations. Provide just those three parts. Ensure deterministic. New algorithm must be totally different form but motivated from them. Provide algorithm description in braces: maybe \\"Start from empty set, process numbers in order of decreasing binary weight, adding if safe, then perform simulated annealing swaps.\\" Provide code implementing that. Must ensure returns list/tuple. We\'ll produce deterministic RNG.\\n\\nImplementation: generate order based on number of 1 bits (popcount) descending, then random tie-breaker using rng. Greedy add, then local improvement with pairwise swaps limited. Ensure AP-free check efficient: maintain set.\\n\\nImplement can_add similar. For improvement, attempt to replace one element with two new ones? but simple pairwise swap: try each non-in set element, find a conflict set to replace maybe up to 2 for net gain. Simpler: after greedy, do random attempts limited to 5000: pick x not in S, if can add, add; else try to remove one conflict element y that blocks x and see if after removal x can be added and total size not decrease. Keep if size same or increase.\\n\\nWe\'ll produce code.\\n\\nLet\'s craft answer.","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to produce answer: identify common backbone idea, describe new algorithm in one sentence inside braces, then provide code implementation. Must not give extra explanations. Provide just those three parts. Ensure deterministic. New algorithm must be totally different form but motivated from them. Provide algorithm description in braces: maybe \\"Start from empty set, process numbers in order of decreasing binary weight, adding if safe, then perform simulated annealing swaps.\\" Provide code implementing that. Must ensure returns list/tuple. We\'ll produce deterministic RNG.\\n\\nImplementation: generate order based on number of 1 bits (popcount) descending, then random tie-breaker using rng. Greedy add, then local improvement with pairwise swaps limited. Ensure AP-free check efficient: maintain set.\\n\\nImplement can_add similar. For improvement, attempt to replace one element with two new ones? but simple pairwise swap: try each non-in set element, find a conflict set to replace maybe up to 2 for net gain. Simpler: after greedy, do random attempts limited to 5000: pick x not in S, if can add, add; else try to remove one conflict element y that blocks x and see if after removal x can be added and total size not decrease. Keep if size same or increase.\\n\\nWe\'ll produce code.\\n\\nLet\'s craft answer."}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1164,"prompt_tokens":2642,"total_tokens":3806}}'
