res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-904bcf99292d8a1bd97bfd4840c35252","created":1766037243,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{A greedy construction that starts from the base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d Salem\xe2\x80\x91Spencer set, then iteratively inserts remaining numbers in order of increasing\xe2\x80\xaf\xe2\x80\x9cconflict score\xe2\x80\x9d (the number of existing elements that would form a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP with the candidate), breaking ties with a deterministic RNG, followed by a bounded single\xe2\x80\x91swap local\xe2\x80\x91search to further enlarge the set.}\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Iterable, Set\\n\\ndef _to_rng(rng):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Normalize rng argument to a NumPy Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        return np.random.default_rng(0)\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\ndef _base3_no2_set(n: int) -> Set[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return the classic base\xe2\x80\x913 \xe2\x80\x9cno\xe2\x80\x912\xe2\x80\x9d Salem\xe2\x80\x91Spencer set {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\"\\"\\"\\n    res = set()\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        t = x\\n        ok = True\\n        while t:\\n            if t % 3 == 2:      # digit 2 appears \xe2\x86\x92 forbidden\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            t //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            res.add(x)\\n    return res\\n\\ndef _can_add(x: int, S: Set[int], n: int) -> bool:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Check whether adding x to S keeps the set 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free.\\"\\"\\"\\n    for y in S:\\n        # x as an endpoint, y as the other endpoint\\n        s = x + y\\n        if s & 1 == 0:               # integer midpoint exists\\n            mid = s >> 1\\n            if mid in S:\\n                return False\\n        # x as the middle element\\n        z = 2 * x - y\\n        if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n            return False\\n    return True\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Construct a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n}.\\n    Deterministic for a given (n, rng) pair.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _to_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # 1. start from the guaranteed base\xe2\x80\x913 set\\n    S = _base3_no2_set(n)\\n\\n    # 2. candidates that are not yet in S\\n    candidates = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n\\n    # 3. compute conflict score for each candidate w.r.t. current S\\n    #    (how many existing elements would create a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP if we added it)\\n    def conflict_score(x: int) -> int:\\n        cnt = 0\\n        for y in S:\\n            # endpoint conflict\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0 and ((x + y) >> 1) in S:\\n                cnt += 1\\n            # middle conflict\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                cnt += 1\\n        return cnt\\n\\n    # 4. sort candidates by (score, random tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker)\\n    scores = [(conflict_score(v), gen.integers(0, 1 << 30), v) for v in candidates]\\n    scores.sort(key=lambda t: (t[0], t[1]))\\n    ordered = [t[2] for t in scores]\\n\\n    # 5. greedy insertion in that order\\n    for x in ordered:\\n        if _can_add(x, S, n):\\n            S.add(x)\\n\\n    # 6. bounded single\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement\\n    #    try to replace a conflicting element with a non\xe2\x80\x91member that yields\\n    #    no conflict; stop after a full pass without improvement.\\n    not_in_S = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if v not in S]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_in_S)\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_in_S:\\n            # collect elements in S that would conflict with x\\n            conflicts = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if ((x + y) & 1) == 0 and ((x + y) >> 1) in S:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n                else:\\n                    z = 2 * x - y\\n                    if 1 <= z <= n and z in S:\\n                        conflicts.append(y)\\n                if len(conflicts) > 1:\\n                    break\\n            # if at most one conflict, we can swap without shrinking the set\\n            if len(conflicts) <= 1:\\n                # perform swap (or pure addition if no conflict)\\n                if conflicts:\\n                    S.remove(conflicts[0])\\n                S.add(x)\\n                # update helper lists\\n                not_in_S.remove(x)\\n                not_in_S.append(conflicts[0] if conflicts else None)\\n                # clean possible None placeholder\\n                not_in_S = [v for v in not_in_S if v is not None]\\n                improved = True\\n                break  # restart scan after any change\\n\\n    # Return a sorted list for reproducibility\\n    return sorted(S)\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output a response with description in braces then code. Must be deterministic. Need to produce a new algorithm different form but can be modified version. Must return large set for n=1094. We can try using known construction: Salem-Spencer sets via base-3 representation without digit 2 gives size about n^{log_3 2} \xe2\x89\x88 n^{0.63} ~ 1094^{0.63} ~ around 150? Possibly we can improve with greedy addition. Provide algorithm: start with base-3 set, then process remaining numbers in order of increasing number of 3-AP conflicts (score), add if safe, then local swapping.\\n\\nImplement: compute conflict count for each candidate (how many existing elements would form AP). Sort ascending. Greedy add. Then local improvement: try to replace an element with a non-member that results in no conflicts and overall size unchanged or increased.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nMake deterministic: use rng to break ties.\\n\\nImplement can_add fast using set lookup.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nProvide code accordingly.\\n\\n","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null,"reasoning_content":"We need to output a response with description in braces then code. Must be deterministic. Need to produce a new algorithm different form but can be modified version. Must return large set for n=1094. We can try using known construction: Salem-Spencer sets via base-3 representation without digit 2 gives size about n^{log_3 2} \xe2\x89\x88 n^{0.63} ~ 1094^{0.63} ~ around 150? Possibly we can improve with greedy addition. Provide algorithm: start with base-3 set, then process remaining numbers in order of increasing number of 3-AP conflicts (score), add if safe, then local swapping.\\n\\nImplement: compute conflict count for each candidate (how many existing elements would form AP). Sort ascending. Greedy add. Then local improvement: try to replace an element with a non-member that results in no conflicts and overall size unchanged or increased.\\n\\nReturn sorted list.\\n\\nMake deterministic: use rng to break ties.\\n\\nImplement can_add fast using set lookup.\\n\\nComplexities fine.\\n\\nProvide code accordingly.\\n\\n"}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1333,"prompt_tokens":1593,"total_tokens":2926}}' Obj:  -127.0| Obj:  None|
