res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-886fa4dc7d1cacd3ea352490f7e7e6a3","created":1766037255,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Union\\n\\ndef _make_rng(rng: Union[None, int, np.random.Generator]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a deterministic NumPy Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        rng = 0\\n    return np.random.default_rng(rng)\\n\\n\\ndef _ternary_no2(n: int) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Numbers in 1..n whose base\xe2\x80\x913 representation uses only digits 0 or 1.\\"\\"\\"\\n    res = []\\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\\n        t = x\\n        ok = True\\n        while t:\\n            if t % 3 == 2:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n            t //= 3\\n        if ok:\\n            res.append(x)\\n    return res\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    {Build a Salem\xe2\x80\x93Spencer set by first taking all numbers with only 0/1 ternary digits,\\n    then greedily inserting the rest ordered by a cheap conflict score, finally applying\\n    a single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement.}\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _make_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # --- 1. seed set from ternary representation (AP\xe2\x80\x91free by construction) ---\\n    seed = _ternary_no2(n)\\n    present = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=bool)\\n    for v in seed:\\n        present[v] = True\\n    S = seed.copy()                     # current solution list\\n\\n    # --- helper to test insertion ---\\n    def can_insert(x: int) -> bool:\\n        for y in S:\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                m = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[m]:\\n                    return False\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # --- 2. compute cheap conflict score for every remaining element ----------\\n    remaining = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n\\n    def conflict_score(x: int) -> int:\\n        cnt = 0\\n        for y in S:\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                m = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[m]:\\n                    cnt += 1\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                cnt += 1\\n        return cnt\\n\\n    scores = np.empty(len(remaining), dtype=int)\\n    for i, v in enumerate(remaining):\\n        scores[i] = conflict_score(v)\\n\\n    # random tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker\\n    order = list(range(len(remaining)))\\n    gen.shuffle(order)\\n\\n    # stable sort by (score, random order)\\n    order.sort(key=lambda i: (scores[i], i))\\n    greedy_order = [remaining[i] for i in order]\\n\\n    # --- 3. greedy insertion --------------------------------------------------\\n    for x in greedy_order:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n            S.append(x)\\n\\n    # --- 4. single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement ------------------------------------\\n    not_present = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_present)\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_present:\\n            # find S\xe2\x80\x91elements that would conflict with x\\n            conflicts = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                    m = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if present[m]:\\n                        conflicts.append(y)\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n                if len(conflicts) > 1:\\n                    break\\n            if len(conflicts) == 1:\\n                rem = conflicts[0]\\n                # perform swap\\n                present[rem] = False\\n                S.remove(rem)\\n\\n                present[x] = True\\n                S.append(x)\\n\\n                not_present.remove(x)\\n                not_present.append(rem)\\n\\n                improved = True\\n                break   # restart scan\\n\\n    # --- 5. final greedy pass on leftovers ------------------------------------\\n    leftovers = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(leftovers)\\n    for x in leftovers:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n            S.append(x)\\n\\n    S.sort()\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to output code only, no explanations, just implement new algorithm with different parameter settings of score function. It must produce a large Salem-Spencer set for n=1094. Need deterministic.\\n\\nWe can design algorithm using base-3 representation: known construction: take numbers whose ternary representation contains only digits 0 and 1 (no 2). This gives size about n^{log_3 2} \xe2\x89\x88 n^{0.631}. For n=1094, count? Let\'s compute quickly manually? Not needed; just implement. This set is known to be AP-free (since no 3-term AP in such set). Actually it\'s known that set of numbers with no digit 2 in base 3 is AP-free (Stanley). So we can produce that set, which may be near optimal. Could also apply greedy addition after that.\\n\\nImplement score differently: maybe compute \\"max gap\\" etc. But we just need different parameter settings.\\n\\nWe\'ll produce algorithm: first select numbers with base-3 digits only 0/1 (call S0). Then run greedy insertion of remaining numbers in random order prioritized by low conflict count (like original but different scoring). Then optional local swap.\\n\\nSimplify: Use base-3 construction then greedy add leftover in increasing order of conflict score but we can compute score as number of existing AP conflicts with S0 maybe.\\n\\nImplement:\\n\\n- Convert rn