res.status = 200, body = b'{"id":"chatcmpl-8c591c4920486395c137cb834999ff11","created":1766037255,"model":"hosted_vllm/openai/gpt-oss-120b","object":"chat.completion","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"{Greedy construction that prioritises numbers with few ternary \xe2\x80\x9c2\xe2\x80\x9d\xe2\x80\x91digits (i.e., low 2\xe2\x80\x91adic ternary score), followed by a single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement to enlarge the 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free set.}  \\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom typing import List, Union\\n\\ndef _make_rng(rng: Union[None, int, np.random.Generator]) -> np.random.Generator:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Return a deterministic NumPy Generator.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator):\\n        return rng\\n    if rng is None:\\n        rng = 0\\n    return np.random.default_rng(int(rng))\\n\\n\\ndef _ternary_two_score(x: int) -> int:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Count how many ternary digits of x are equal to 2.\\"\\"\\"\\n    cnt = 0\\n    while x:\\n        if x % 3 == 2:\\n            cnt += 1\\n        x //= 3\\n    return cnt\\n\\n\\ndef heuristic(n: int, rng=None) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Build a large 3\xe2\x80\x91AP\xe2\x80\x91free subset of {1,\xe2\x80\xa6,n} (intended for n=1094).\\n    The algorithm:\\n      1. rank numbers by fewest ternary\xe2\x80\x912 digits (tie\xe2\x80\x91break randomly);\\n      2. greedy insert respecting the AP\xe2\x80\x91free condition;\\n      3. single\xe2\x80\x91swap local improvement;\\n      4. final greedy pass on remaining numbers.\\n    Returns a sorted list S.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    gen = _make_rng(rng)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 1. Compute the ternary\xe2\x80\x912 score for every element.\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    score = np.fromiter((_ternary_two_score(i) for i in range(n + 1)),\\n                       dtype=np.int16, count=n + 1)\\n\\n    candidates = list(range(1, n + 1))\\n    gen.shuffle(candidates)                     # random tie\xe2\x80\x91breaker\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: score[x])     # low score first\\n\\n    present = np.zeros(n + 1, dtype=bool)\\n    S = []\\n\\n    def can_insert(x: int) -> bool:\\n        \\"\\"\\"Check whether x can be added without creating a 3\xe2\x80\x91AP.\\"\\"\\"\\n        for y in S:\\n            # x and y as endpoints\\n            if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                if present[mid]:\\n                    return False\\n            # x as middle\\n            z = 2 * x - y\\n            if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 2. Greedy insertion according to the ternary\xe2\x80\x912 priority.\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    for x in candidates:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n            S.append(x)\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 3. Single\xe2\x80\x91swap improvement: replace a conflicting element if it\\n    #    yields a net gain (or equal size with a more \xe2\x80\x9cpromising\xe2\x80\x9d element).\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    not_present = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(not_present)\\n\\n    improved = True\\n    while improved:\\n        improved = False\\n        for x in not_present:\\n            # collect S\xe2\x80\x91elements that would violate the AP\xe2\x80\x91free rule with x\\n            conflicts = []\\n            for y in S:\\n                if ((x + y) & 1) == 0:\\n                    mid = (x + y) >> 1\\n                    if present[mid]:\\n                        conflicts.append(y)\\n                z = 2 * x - y\\n                if 1 <= z <= n and present[z]:\\n                    conflicts.append(y)\\n                if len(conflicts) > 1:\\n                    break\\n            if len(conflicts) == 1:\\n                # perform the swap\\n                rem = conflicts[0]\\n                present[rem] = False\\n                S.remove(rem)\\n\\n                present[x] = True\\n                S.append(x)\\n\\n                not_present.remove(x)\\n                not_present.append(rem)\\n\\n                improved = True\\n                break   # restart scanning after any modification\\n\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    # 4. Final greedy pass over the leftovers (they may now fit).\\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------\\n    leftovers = [v for v in range(1, n + 1) if not present[v]]\\n    gen.shuffle(leftovers)\\n    for x in leftovers:\\n        if can_insert(x):\\n            present[x] = True\\n            S.append(x)\\n\\n    S.sort()\\n    return S\\n```","role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need to comply with policy: must not claim to find the largest possible set, but can attempt. Provide code. Ensure deterministic. Use different score function than original. Perhaps weight by number of powers of 2 residue classes.\\n\\nImplement algorithm: rank candidates by number of 2-power divisibility (i.e., exponent of 2 in factorization) descending to prefer numbers with high 2-adic valuation because they are in known Salem-Spencer set (Salem-Spencer construction using base-3 representation with digits 0 or 1). Actually known construction: choose numbers whose base-3 representation contains no digit 2: size about n^{log_3 2} ~ n^{0.63}. For n=1094, we can compute such set exactly; it\'s maximal known is around maybe 180? Let\'s compute. Let\'s produce set of numbers <=1094 with ternary digits only 0/1. That yields a 3-AP-free set (classical). That\'s deterministic and likely large. That might be acceptable.\\n\\nThe request: \\"d